window.CURRICULUM = [

{t:"Start Here", ls:[
["What is machine learning?",
"In normal programming you write the rules. In machine learning you show the computer lots of examples and it learns the rules itself.\n\nA spam filter isn’t told ‘block emails containing FREE’. It sees thousands of emails labeled spam or not spam and learns which patterns matter.",
[["In machine learning, where do the rules come from?",["Learned from example data","Hand-written as if-then rules by experts","Looked up in a fixed database of rules","Set by the user each time the model runs"]],
 ["Which task is a good fit for ML?",["Recognizing cats in photos","Adding two numbers exactly","Sorting a list alphabetically","Converting Celsius to Fahrenheit"],"Hard to write rules for, easy to collect examples."],
 ["An ML engineer’s job is mostly about…",["Data, training and reliable production models","Inventing new optimizers and proving theorems","Writing research papers on new algorithms","Hand-writing rules for each business case"]]]]
]},

{t:"Python Refresher", ls:[
["Where Python Runs",
"Python code runs in an interpreter. You can run a file with `python train.py`, type lines into the interactive REPL, or run cells in a Jupyter notebook, a popular choice for ML experiments.\n\nNotebooks keep variables in memory between cells, so running cells out of order can cause confusing bugs.",
[["What’s the main risk of running notebook cells out of order?",["Variables may hold stale or unexpected values","The kernel crashes and loses your work","Python raises a SyntaxError on that cell","The notebook re-sorts the cells into top-down order"]],
 ["Command to run a script called train.py?",["`python train.py`","`run train.py`","`open train.py`","`python -run train`"]],
 ["Why do ML folks love notebooks?",["You see results and plots inline, step by step","They run code faster than plain scripts","PyTorch only runs inside a notebook","They prevent the stale-state bugs that scripts get"]]]],
["Variables and Types",
"A variable is a name pointing to a value. Python figures out the type for you.\n\n$x = 3        # int\ny = 2.5      # float\nname = 'ada' # str\nok = True    # bool\n\nUse `type(x)` to check a type.",
[["What is `type(2.5)`?",["float","int","str","double"]],
 ["Which value is a bool?",["`False`","`'False'`","`0.0`","`None`"],"Quotes make it a string."],
 ["After `a = 5` then `a = 'hi'`, what is `a`?",["`'hi'`","An error, since `a` was an int","`5`","`[5, 'hi']`"],"A name can point to any type; reassigning just moves the name to the new value."]]],
["Numbers That Change",
"Arithmetic uses `+ - * /`. Division `/` on ints or floats always gives a float. `//` is floor division, `%` is the remainder, and `**` is power.\n\n$7 / 2   # 3.5\n7 // 2  # 3\n7 % 2   # 1\n2 ** 3  # 8\n\n`x += 1` is shorthand for `x = x + 1`.",
[["`10 // 3` equals?",["3","3.33","1","4"]],
 ["`10 % 3` equals?",["1","3","0","3.33"]],
 ["`2 ** 4` equals?",["16","8","6","32"]]]],
["When Python Complains",
"Errors tell you what went wrong. Read the last line of the traceback first.\n\n`NameError`: you used a name that doesn’t exist. `TypeError`: wrong type, like `'3' + 4`. `IndexError`: list index out of range. `KeyError`: missing dict key.\n\nCatch errors you expect with `try` and `except`.",
[["`'3' + 4` raises…",["TypeError","NameError","KeyError","ValueError"]],
 ["Where should you look first in a long traceback?",["The last line","The first line","The middle frame","Your own top-level call"]],
 ["Which block runs if the code in `try` fails?",["`except`","`try`","`else`","`raise`"]]]],
["Strings and f-strings",
"Strings hold text. f-strings drop values into text.\n\n$acc = 0.9312\nprint(f'accuracy: {acc:.2f}')  # accuracy: 0.93\n\nUseful methods: `.lower()`, `.strip()`, `.split()`, `.replace()`.",
[["`f'{0.5:.1%}'` gives?",["`50.0%`","`0.5%`","`0.50%`","`50%`"]],
 ["`'a,b,c'.split(',')` gives?",["`['a', 'b', 'c']`","`'abc'`","`('a', 'b', 'c')`","`['a,b,c']`"]],
 ["`'  hi  '.strip()` gives?",["`'hi'`","`'  hi'`","`'hi  '`","`' hi '`"]]]],
["Comparisons and Booleans",
"Comparisons return `True` or `False`: `==`, `!=`, `<`, `>=`. Combine them with `and`, `or`, `not`.\n\n`=` assigns, `==` compares. Zero, `None` and empty containers (`0`, `None`, `''`, `[]`) count as False in an `if`.",
[["`3 > 2 and 1 > 5` is?",["False","True","An error","`None`"]],
 ["Which is falsy?",["`[]`","`[0]`","`'0'`","`' '`"],"A non-empty list or string is truthy, even if it contains 0."],
 ["Which operator checks equality?",["`==`","`=`","`:=`","`is`"]]]],
["If, Elif, Else",
"Branches run the first block whose condition matches.\n\n$if score >= 0.9:\n    grade = 'A'\nelif score >= 0.8:\n    grade = 'B'\nelse:\n    grade = 'C'\n\nIndentation (4 spaces by convention) defines each block.",
[["With `score = 0.85`, what is `grade`?",["B","A","C","`None`"]],
 ["What defines a block in Python?",["Indentation","Curly braces","The `elif`/`else` keywords","A colon at the end of each block"]],
 ["Can you have several `elif` branches?",["Yes, as many as you need","No, only one per `if`","Only if there’s an `else`","Up to three, then use `match`"]]]],
["Lists and Indexing",
"Lists are ordered, changeable collections. Indexes start at 0, and negative indexes count from the end.\n\n$xs = [10, 20, 30]\nxs[0]    # 10\nxs[-1]   # 30\nxs.append(40)\nlen(xs)  # 4",
[["`[5, 6, 7][1]` is?",["6","5","7","An error"]],
 ["`[5, 6, 7][-1]` is?",["7","5","6","An error"]],
 ["How do you add an item to the end of a list?",["`xs.append(x)`","`xs.add(x)`","`xs.push(x)`","`xs.insert(x)`"]]]],
["Slicing and Changing Lists",
"Slices grab a range: `xs[start:stop]` includes start and excludes stop.\n\n$xs = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]\nxs[1:3]   # [1, 2]\nxs[:2]    # [0, 1]\nxs[::-1]  # reversed\n\nAssigning a list to a new name doesn’t copy it. Use `xs.copy()` or `xs[:]`.",
[["`[0, 1, 2, 3, 4][2:4]` is?",["`[2, 3]`","`[2, 3, 4]`","`[1, 2]`","`[3, 4]`"]],
 ["After `b = a` and `b.append(1)`, does `a` change?",["Yes, both names share one list","No, `b = a` makes a copy","Only if `a` was empty","Only if `a` is a global variable"]],
 ["Reverse a list with a slice?",["`xs[::-1]`","`xs[-1:]`","`xs[1::]`","`xs[-1::1]`"]]]],
["Loops That Do Something",
"`for` loops walk over any iterable.\n\n$total = 0\nfor loss in losses:\n    total += loss\navg = total / len(losses)\n\nA common pattern: loop, update an accumulator, then use the result.",
[["`total = 0`, then `for loss in losses: total += loss`, then `avg = total / len(losses)`. What is `avg`?",["The mean of `losses`","The largest value in `losses`","The last value in `losses`","The sum of `losses`"]],
 ["`for c in 'abc':` runs how many times?",["3","1","0","4"]],
 ["Which of these can a for loop walk over?",["Lists, strings, dicts, ranges","Only lists, tuples and arrays","Only a `range(n)` of numbers","Only sequences with an index"]]]],
["Range, Enumerate, Zip",
"`range(n)` gives 0 to n−1. `enumerate` gives the index and the item. `zip` walks several lists side by side.\n\n$for i, x in enumerate(['a', 'b']):\n    print(i, x)   # 0 a, then 1 b\nfor x, y in zip(X, Y):\n    ...\n\n`zip` stops at the shortest list.",
[["`list(range(3))` is?",["`[0, 1, 2]`","`[1, 2, 3]`","`[0, 1, 2, 3]`","`[3]`"]],
 ["Zipping lists of length 3 and 5 gives how many pairs?",["3","5","8","15"]],
 ["You need the index and the value while looping. Use…",["`enumerate`","`zip`","`range`","`reversed`"]]]],
["While, Break, Continue",
"`while` repeats as long as a condition is True. `break` exits the loop, and `continue` skips to the next round.\n\n$epoch = 0\nwhile True:\n    epoch += 1\n    if val_loss_went_up():\n        break\n\nIf nothing ever makes the condition False, you get an infinite loop.",
[["What does `continue` do?",["Skips the rest of this round","Exits the loop entirely","Restarts the loop from round one","Pauses until the condition is True"]],
 ["A natural use of `break` in training?",["Early stopping","Loading each batch","Printing the loss","Shuffling the data"]],
 ["`while x < 3:` where `x` never changes and starts at 0 →",["Infinite loop","Runs once","Syntax error","Runs 3 times, then stops"]]]],
["Comprehensions",
"A comprehension builds a collection in one line.\n\n$squares = [x**2 for x in range(5)]\nevens = [x for x in nums if x % 2 == 0]\nlengths = {w: len(w) for w in words}  # a dict\n\nRead it as: give me this, for each item, if a condition holds.",
[["`[x * 2 for x in [1, 2, 3]]` is?",["`[2, 4, 6]`","`[1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 3]`","`6`","`[1, 4, 9]`"]],
 ["`[x for x in range(6) if x % 3 == 0]` is?",["`[0, 3]`","`[3]`","`[0, 3, 6]`","`[3, 6]`"]],
 ["Curly braces with `key: value` inside make a…",["dict","set","list","tuple"]]]],
["Dicts and Lookups",
"Dicts map keys to values with fast lookup.\n\n$cfg = {'lr': 3e-4, 'epochs': 10}\ncfg['lr']            # 0.0003\ncfg.get('bs', 32)    # 32 if missing\nfor k, v in cfg.items():\n    ...\n\nA missing key with `[]` raises KeyError; `.get()` returns a default.",
[["`{'a': 1}.get('b', 0)` is?",["0","KeyError","None","`'b'`"]],
 ["Loop over keys and values with…",["`d.items()`","`d.pairs()`","`d.keys()`","`d.values()`"]],
 ["How fast is looking up a key in a dict, typically?",["Constant time on average","Linear in the number of keys","Logarithmic, like a sorted tree","Depends on insertion order"]]]],
["Tuples and Nested Data",
"Tuples are like lists but can’t be changed: `shape = (32, 3, 224, 224)`. They’re great for fixed records and can be unpacked.\n\n$h, w = (224, 224)\nbatch = [{'x': [1, 2], 'y': 0}, {'x': [3, 4], 'y': 1}]\nbatch[1]['x'][0]   # 3\n\nTuples of hashable values can be dict keys; lists can’t.",
[["`t = (1, 2)` then `t[0] = 5` →",["A TypeError","`t` becomes `(5, 2)`","Nothing happens","A new tuple `(5, 2)` is returned"]],
 ["With `batch = [{'x': [1, 2], 'y': 0}, {'x': [3, 4], 'y': 1}]`, what is `batch[0]['y']`?",["0","1","`[1, 2]`","3"]],
 ["Which can be a dict key?",["`(1, 2)`","`[1, 2]`","`{1: 2}`","`{1, 2}`"]]]],
["Functions and Returns",
"Functions package reusable logic. `return` sends a value back; without it, the function returns `None`.\n\n$def accuracy(preds, labels):\n    correct = sum(p == y for p, y in zip(preds, labels))\n    return correct / len(labels)\n\nVariables created inside a function stay local to it.",
[["A function with no `return` gives back…",["`None`","`0`","An error","An empty string"]],
 ["`accuracy(preds, labels)` returns the fraction of positions where they match. What is `accuracy([1, 0, 1], [1, 1, 1])`?",["About 0.67","1.0","About 0.33","2"]],
 ["Can code outside a function see a variable created inside it?",["No, it’s local to the function","Yes, always","Only if it was printed","Only after the function returns"]]]],
["Arguments With Names",
"Arguments can have defaults and can be passed by name.\n\n$def train(model, lr=1e-3, epochs=5):\n    ...\ntrain(m, epochs=20)\n\n`*args` collects extra positional arguments and `**kwargs` collects extra named ones. Avoid mutable defaults like `def f(x=[])`, because the same list is shared across calls.",
[["Given `def train(model, lr=1e-3, epochs=5)`, after `train(m, epochs=20)` what is `lr`?",["1e-3","20","None","5"]],
 ["`**kwargs` collects…",["Extra named arguments, as a dict","Extra positional arguments, as a tuple","All arguments, as a list","Default values, as a dict"]],
 ["Why avoid `def f(x=[])`?",["The same list is shared across calls","Lists can’t be used as defaults","Defaults are re-created on every call","Mutable defaults can’t be passed by name"]]]],
["Objects, Methods, Attributes",
"A class bundles data (attributes) with behavior (methods). PyTorch models are classes.\n\n$class Counter:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.n = 0\n    def add(self):\n        self.n += 1\nc = Counter(); c.add(); c.n   # 1\n\n`self` is the object itself, and `__init__` runs when it’s created.",
[["What runs when you create `Counter()`?",["`__init__`","`add`","`__main__`","`__call__`"]],
 ["`c.n` is…",["An attribute","A method","A module","A class variable"]],
 ["In PyTorch, you define a model by…",["Subclassing `nn.Module` with a `forward`","Writing a dict of layer names","Calling `torch.model()` with a config","Writing a plain function that holds the weights"]]]],
["NumPy Arrays",
"NumPy arrays are fast grids of numbers. Math applies element by element, and broadcasting stretches smaller shapes to match.\n\n$import numpy as np\na = np.array([[1, 2], [3, 4]])\na.shape          # (2, 2)\na * 10           # every element times 10\na @ a            # matrix multiply\na.mean(axis=0)   # column means\n\nVectorized NumPy code is far faster than Python loops.",
[["Shape of `np.zeros((3, 4))`?",["(3, 4)","(4, 3)","12","(12,)"]],
 ["`a @ b` means…",["Matrix multiplication","Element-wise multiplication","A decorator","Outer product"]],
 ["`a.mean(axis=0)` on a (100, 5) array has shape…",["(5,)","(100,)","()","(1, 5)"]]]],
["Pandas DataFrames",
"pandas holds tables. Rows are examples, columns are features.\n\n$import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv('users.csv')\ndf.head()\ndf['age'].mean()\ndf[df['churned'] == 1]\ndf.groupby('plan')['spend'].mean()\ndf.isna().sum()   # missing values per column",
[["Select rows where `churned` is 1?",["`df[df['churned'] == 1]`","`df.filter(churned == 1)`","`df['churned'] == 1`","`df.get('churned', 1)`"]],
 ["Count missing values per column?",["`df.isna().sum()`","`df.count_na()`","`df.isna()`","`df.dropna().count()`"]],
 ["Average spend per plan?",["`df.groupby('plan')['spend'].mean()`","`df.mean('plan')`","`df['plan'].spend()`","`df.groupby('spend')['plan'].mean()`"]]]]
]},

{t:"Foundations", ls:[
["What is a Model?",
"A model is a function with adjustable numbers, called parameters, that maps inputs to outputs. Training means finding parameter values that make the outputs match the data.\n\nPredicting house price as `w × size + b` is a model with two parameters, w and b.",
[["Training a model means…",["Tuning its parameters to fit the data","Writing its if-then rules by hand","Choosing which inputs to collect","Running it on new examples to get predictions"]],
 ["In price = w × size + b, the parameters are…",["w and b","size and price","Only size","w, b and size"]],
 ["Using a trained model on new inputs is called…",["Inference","Backprop","Labeling","Fine-tuning"]]]],
["Supervised vs Unsupervised",
"Supervised learning has labeled answers to learn from, like spam or not spam, or a house’s sale price. Unsupervised learning finds structure without labels, like grouping similar customers.\n\nSelf-supervised learning creates its own labels from raw data, like predicting the next word. That’s how LLMs are pretrained.",
[["Grouping customers with no labels is…",["Unsupervised","Supervised","Reinforcement learning","Self-supervised"]],
 ["Predicting price from labeled past sales is…",["Supervised","Unsupervised","Self-supervised","Reinforcement learning"]],
 ["LLM next-word pretraining is…",["Self-supervised","Unsupervised clustering","Reinforcement learning","Supervised, with human labels"]]]],
["Features and Labels",
"Features are the inputs: age, plan, logins last week. The label is what you want to predict: did they churn?\n\nGood features carry signal about the label and are actually available at prediction time. Predicting a number is regression; predicting a category is classification.",
[["Predicting churn, ‘logins last week’ is a…",["Feature","Label","Parameter","Hyperparameter"]],
 ["Which feature is a problem for churn prediction?",["‘account closed date’","‘signup month’","‘number of support tickets’","‘logins in the last 30 days’"],"It reveals the answer. That’s leakage."],
 ["Predicting a price is…",["Regression","Classification","Clustering","Ranking"]]]],
["Data Preprocessing",
"Raw data needs cleaning. Handle missing values (drop or fill), encode categories (one-hot), and scale numbers so features sit on similar ranges, for example standardizing to mean 0 and standard deviation 1.\n\nFit scalers on the training data only, then apply them to validation and test.",
[["Turning plan = basic / pro / team into numbers usually uses…",["One-hot encoding","Standardizing to mean 0","Mapping to 1, 2, 3 and scaling","Filling with the column mean"],"One-hot avoids implying that team is ‘three times’ basic, which a 1/2/3 code does."],
 ["Fit the scaler on…",["Training data only","All data, including test","Test data only","Training plus validation and test"]],
 ["Why scale features?",["So big-range features don’t dominate","To make the dataset smaller on disk","To remove outliers from every numeric column","Because tree models can’t split unscaled values"]]]],
["Train / Test Split",
"Split your data: train to fit, validation to tune, test for one final honest score. Common rule-of-thumb splits are 70/15/15 or 80/10/10.\n\nFor time-based data, split by time. When one user or patient has many rows, keep all their rows in the same split.",
[["Which set is used once, at the very end?",["Test","Validation","Train","Whichever split is smallest"]],
 ["The same patient appears in both train and test →",["Leakage: the test score is optimistic","That’s fine if the rows differ","It improves generalization","It only matters for small datasets with few patients"]],
 ["For forecasting, how should you split?",["By time: past for train, future for test","Randomly, stratified by the target label","By user, with all rows per user together","Randomly, then sort each split by date"]]]],
["How a Model Actually Learns",
"The learning loop: make predictions, measure how wrong they are with a loss, compute how each parameter affects the loss (the gradient), then nudge the parameters to reduce it. Repeat thousands of times. One full pass over the training set is an epoch; training often runs for several.",
[["What measures how wrong predictions are?",["The loss function","The learning rate","The gradient","The optimizer"]],
 ["The gradient tells you…",["How to change each weight to lower the loss","How accurate the model will be at the end","Which training examples the model got wrong most","How large each update step should be"]],
 ["One pass through the whole training set is…",["An epoch","A batch","A step","An iteration"]]]],
["Draw the Best Line",
"Linear regression fits y = w·x + b. ‘Best’ means the line with the smallest total error between predictions and true points, usually mean squared error (MSE).\n\nWith many features it becomes y = w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ + … + b.",
[["Linear regression predicts…",["A number","A category","A cluster ID","A probability between 0 and 1"]],
 ["Common loss for linear regression?",["Mean squared error","Cross-entropy","Accuracy","Hinge loss with a margin"]],
 ["A positive weight on ‘size’ means…",["Bigger size → higher predicted price","Size has no effect on the price","Bigger size → lower predicted price","Size is the most important feature overall"]]]],
["How We Score a Wrong Answer",
"A loss turns wrongness into a number. MSE squares the errors, so big misses hurt a lot. MAE uses absolute errors, so it’s more forgiving of outliers.\n\nCross-entropy is for predicted probabilities, like the sigmoid outputs in the next lesson: it punishes confident wrong answers heavily.",
[["Which loss is more robust to outliers?",["MAE","MSE","They’re equal","RMSE"]],
 ["A confident wrong classification gives a cross-entropy loss that is…",["Very large","Zero","Negative","Slightly above zero"]],
 ["Squaring errors makes big mistakes…",["Count much more","Count less","Count the same","Become negative"]]]],
["From Line to Probability",
"Logistic regression passes a line through the sigmoid, σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e⁻ᶻ), giving a value between 0 and 1 that acts as a probability. Predict class 1 above a threshold, often 0.5.\n\nFor many classes, softmax turns a vector of scores into probabilities that sum to 1.\n\nThe raw score z = w·x + b that goes into the sigmoid is called the logit, or log-odds: z = 0 means 50/50, and each +1 in z multiplies the odds by e ≈ 2.7. Softmax turns a vector of logits into probabilities: softmax([2, 0, 0]) ≈ [0.79, 0.11, 0.11]. Recall is the share of real positives you catch; precision is the share of your positive calls that are right. Evaluation Metrics covers both properly.",
[["σ(0) equals?",["0.5","0","1","0.25"]],
 ["Softmax outputs…",["Probabilities that sum to 1","Scores each between 0 and 1, independently","A single class above the threshold","Values between −1 and 1"]],
 ["Lowering the threshold from 0.5 to 0.3 tends to…",["Raise recall and lower precision","Raise precision and lower recall","Raise both precision and recall","Change nothing about the errors"]]]],
["Gradient Descent",
"Picture standing on a foggy hill. Feel the slope, step downhill, repeat. That’s gradient descent: w = w − lr × gradient.\n\nMini-batch gradient descent uses a small batch per step. Each step is noisier but far cheaper, and the noise can help escape bad spots.",
[["The update rule is…",["w = w − lr × gradient","w = w + lr × gradient","w = gradient / lr","w = w − gradient / lr"]],
 ["Why use mini-batches?",["Cheaper steps that fit in memory","More exact gradients than the full data","Linear models can’t use full batches","They remove noise from each update"]],
 ["If the gradient is 0, you’re at…",["A flat spot: min, max or saddle","Always the global minimum","Always a local minimum","A point where training diverges"]]]],
["Learning Rate",
"The learning rate is the step size. Too high and the loss bounces or explodes. Too low and training crawls. Schedules like warmup then decay are standard.\n\nA quick trick: try values spaced by 10×, like 1e-2, 1e-3, 1e-4, and compare.",
[["Loss jumps around and goes up →",["The learning rate is too high","The learning rate is too low","The model is underfitting the data","The batch size is too large"]],
 ["Loss decreases extremely slowly →",["The learning rate may be too low","The learning rate is too high","The model is overfitting","The loss has already hit its minimum"]],
 ["Warmup means…",["Ramping the learning rate up from small","Training on the easiest examples first, then hard","Running a few epochs before logging","Starting at a high rate, then decaying"]]]],
["Overfitting, Underfitting & the Bias–Variance Tradeoff",
"Underfitting: the model is too simple and does badly on both train and validation (high bias). Overfitting: great on train, worse on validation, because it memorized noise (high variance).\n\nMore model complexity lowers bias but raises variance. You’re looking for the sweet spot.",
[["Train 60%, validation 58%, and humans score 95% →",["Underfitting","Overfitting","A good fit","Data leakage"]],
 ["Train 99%, validation 75% →",["Overfitting","Underfitting","Definitely leakage","A good fit"]],
 ["Which helps with overfitting?",["More data or regularization","A bigger model","Training for many more epochs","A higher learning rate"]]]],
["Validation Loss & Early Stopping",
"Plot training and validation loss each epoch. Training loss keeps falling, but when validation loss starts rising, the model has started to overfit.\n\nEarly stopping keeps the best checkpoint and stops once validation loss hasn’t improved for a few epochs. That wait is called ‘patience’.",
[["Validation loss rising while training loss falls means…",["Overfitting has started","The model is underfitting","Training loss will soon rise too","The patience counter has reset"]],
 ["In early stopping, ‘patience’ is…",["Epochs to wait for improvement","Epochs before the first checkpoint","How far validation loss may rise","How often a checkpoint is kept"]],
 ["Which checkpoint do you keep?",["The one with lowest validation loss","The last one saved","The one with the lowest training loss","The first one saved"]]]],
["Regularization",
"Regularization discourages overly complex models. L2 adds λΣw² to the loss and shrinks all weights. L1 adds λΣ|w| and pushes many weights to exactly zero.\n\nDropout and data augmentation also regularize. λ sets the strength.",
[["Which makes weights exactly zero?",["L1","L2","Neither","Both"]],
 ["Increasing λ makes the model…",["Simpler and less likely to overfit","More complex, and better at fitting noise","Faster to train every time","Better on the training data"]],
 ["Randomly flipping and cropping training images is…",["Data augmentation","Leakage","Input normalization","Dropout"]]]],
["Cross-Validation",
"K-fold cross-validation splits the data into k parts, trains k times while holding out a different part each time, and averages the scores. It gives a more reliable estimate when data is small.\n\nUse stratified folds for imbalanced classes and time-based splits for time data.",
[["5-fold cross-validation trains how many models?",["5","1","10","4"]],
 ["Its main benefit?",["A steadier score from limited data","Faster training per model","A bigger training set for the final model","No need for a separate test set"]],
 ["Imbalanced classes → use…",["Stratified k-fold","One random 80/20 split","Leave-one-out","Time-based folds"]]]],
["Evaluation Metrics",
"A confusion matrix counts true positives, false positives, false negatives, and true negatives. Precision = TP / (TP + FP). Recall = TP / (TP + FN). F1 = 2PR / (P + R), the harmonic mean of precision and recall, balances both.\n\nFor regression, use MAE, RMSE, or R². Accuracy misleads when classes are imbalanced.",
[["TP = 8, FP = 2. Precision?",["0.8","0.2","0.25","1.0"]],
 ["TP = 8, FN = 8. Recall?",["0.5","0.8","1.0","0.25"]],
 ["1% positives, and the model always says negative. Accuracy?",["99%, but the model is useless","1%, since it never finds a positive","50%, the same as a coin flip","0%, since it misses every positive"]]]],
["Foundations Review — Debug Your First Classifier",
"A debugging checklist: first, try to overfit a tiny batch; if you can’t, you likely have a bug. Check labels and shapes. Compare against a dumb baseline, like always predicting the majority class. Look at errors by hand. And when results look too good, check for leakage.",
[["The model can’t even overfit 10 examples →",["Likely a bug in data, loss or code","It needs much more training data first","It needs stronger regularization","The model is too large to train"]],
 ["99.9% validation accuracy on day one →",["Suspect leakage","Ship it","Lower the learning rate","Add regularization"]],
 ["A first baseline for a classifier?",["Always predict the most common class","Fine-tune a large pretrained model","Tune a big gradient-boosted ensemble","Train a deep net on all the features"],"A trivial rule (majority class, or a random guess) sets the floor that any trained model must beat."]]],
["Spam Classifier",
"Build it end to end: collect labeled emails, turn text into features (bag of words or TF-IDF), train logistic regression or naive Bayes, tune the threshold, and watch precision so real mail isn’t lost.",
[["Simple text features for spam detection?",["TF-IDF word weights","Pixel values","Timestamps only","Sender’s email length"]],
 ["The most costly mistake for users?",["Real email marked as spam","Spam reaching the inbox","Slow training","Low recall on the spam class"],"So you favor precision."],
 ["A good first model?",["Logistic regression or naive Bayes","A bag-of-words lookup with no training","TF-IDF features fed to k-means","A deep CNN trained on raw email bytes"]]]]
]},

{t:"Math Primer", ls:[
["Vectors & Dot Product",
"A vector is a list of numbers, like an embedding [0.2, −1, 0.5]. The dot product multiplies matching entries and adds them: [1, 2]·[3, 4] = 1·3 + 2·4 = 11.\n\nA large positive dot product means the vectors point roughly the same way.",
[["[1, 0, 2]·[2, 5, 1] = ?",["4","8","7","5"]],
 ["Dot product of perpendicular vectors?",["0","1","−1","Their lengths multiplied"]],
 ["Cosine similarity is the dot product of…",["Unit-length (normalized) vectors","Squared, element by element vectors","Mean-centered but unscaled vectors","Vectors sorted by magnitude"]]]],
["Matrices & Shapes",
"A matrix is a grid of numbers. (m×n) @ (n×p) → (m×p): the inner sizes must match. A linear layer computes y = xW + b, where x is (batch, in) and W is (in, out).\n\nShape mismatches are among the most common bugs in deep learning code.",
[["(32×128) @ (128×10) →",["(32×10)","(128×128)","Error","(10×32)"]],
 ["(32×128) @ (64×10) →",["Error","(32×10)","(32×64)","(128×64)"]],
 ["With y = xW + b, a layer maps 512 features to 256. Shape of W?",["(512, 256)","(256, 512)","(256, 256)","(512, 512)"],"With y = xW + b and x of shape (batch, in), W is (in, out). PyTorch’s `nn.Linear` stores the transpose, `weight` of shape (out, in), and computes xWᵀ + b."]]],
["Derivative as Slope",
"A derivative measures how fast the output changes when the input changes: the slope. For f(x) = x², f′(x) = 2x, so at x = 3 the slope is 6.\n\nAt the bottom of a smooth bowl, the slope is 0.",
[["Derivative of x²?",["2x","x","2","x²/2"]],
 ["For f(x) = x², the slope at x = −1?",["−2","2","1","−1"]],
 ["Slope at the bottom of a smooth bowl?",["0","1","Infinity","−1"]]]],
["Partial Derivatives",
"With many inputs, a partial derivative changes one input while holding the others fixed. For f(w, b) = w² + 3b, ∂f/∂w = 2w and ∂f/∂b = 3.\n\nThe gradient is the vector of all partial derivatives. It points uphill.",
[["f = w² + 3b. What is ∂f/∂b?",["3","2w","0","2w + 3"]],
 ["The gradient points…",["Toward the steepest increase","Toward the steepest decrease","Toward the global minimum","Along the contour lines"]],
 ["Gradient descent moves…",["Opposite the gradient","Along the gradient","Perpendicular to the gradient","Toward the largest weight"]]]],
["The Chain Rule",
"When functions are nested, derivatives multiply: if y = f(g(x)), then dy/dx = f′(g(x)) · g′(x).\n\nBackprop is just the chain rule applied layer by layer, from the loss back to each weight.",
[["y = (3x)². What is dy/dx?",["18x","6x","9x","36x"]],
 ["Backprop is an application of…",["The chain rule","Bayes’ rule","The product rule","Taylor’s theorem"]],
 ["The chain rule combines local derivatives by…",["Multiplying them","Adding them","Averaging them","Taking the largest"]]]],
["Probability Basics",
"Probabilities run from 0 to 1. For independent events, P(A and B) = P(A)·P(B). P(not A) = 1 − P(A).\n\nConditional probability P(A | B) is the probability of A given that B happened.",
[["Two fair coin flips, both heads?",["0.25","0.5","0.75","1.0"]],
 ["P(rain) = 0.3. P(no rain)?",["0.7","0.3","1.3","0.09"]],
 ["P(spam | contains ‘free’) is…",["A conditional probability","A joint probability","A marginal (unconditional) probability","A prior probability"]]]],
["Bayes’ Rule",
"P(A | B) = P(B | A)·P(A) / P(B). It flips a conditional around.\n\nClassic trap: a test with 99% sensitivity and 99% specificity, for a disease affecting 1 in 1,000 people, still gives mostly false positives (only about 9% of positives are sick), because healthy people vastly outnumber sick ones.",
[["Bayes’ rule lets you…",["Get P(A | B) from P(B | A)","Get P(A and B) from P(A)","Get P(A) from P(A | B) alone","Treat A and B as independent"]],
 ["Rare disease, accurate test, positive result → most likely…",["Still probably healthy","Almost certainly sick","Exactly 99% likely sick","The test must be broken"],"Healthy people vastly outnumber sick ones, so even a small false-positive rate produces more false positives than true ones."],
 ["In Bayes’ rule, P(A) is called the…",["Prior","Likelihood","Posterior","Evidence"]]]],
["Mean, Variance & Standard Deviation",
"The mean is the average. Variance is the average squared distance from the mean, and standard deviation is its square root, in the same units as the data. That divides by n, for a whole population. For a sample, divide by n − 1 instead (Bessel’s correction) to get an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\nStandardizing: z = (x − mean) / std.",
[["Mean of 2, 4, 6?",["4","6","12","3"]],
 ["Standard deviation of [5, 5, 5]?",["0","5","1","25"]],
 ["z-score of x = 70 with mean 50 and std 10?",["2","20","0.7","−2"]]]],
["Logs and Exponents",
"Logs turn multiplication into addition: log(ab) = log a + log b. That’s why we add log-probabilities instead of multiplying tiny probabilities, which would underflow to zero.\n\nexp undoes log. log(1) = 0, and the log of a number between 0 and 1 is negative.",
[["log(a·b) =",["log a + log b","log a · log b","log(a + b)","log a − log b"]],
 ["Why use log-probabilities?",["Products of tiny numbers underflow","They are always positive","They make GPUs faster","They turn sums into easier products"]],
 ["log(1) =",["0","1","e","−1"]]]]
]},

{t:"Architectures", ls:[
["The Perceptron",
"A perceptron computes a weighted sum of its inputs plus a bias, then applies a step: output 1 if w·x + b > 0, else 0.\n\nIt can only draw a straight line between classes, so a single perceptron can’t solve XOR.",
[["A perceptron’s decision boundary is…",["A straight line (hyperplane)","A step-shaped curve","Any shape, given enough inputs","A circle around one class"]],
 ["A famous problem one perceptron can’t solve?",["XOR","AND","OR","NOT"]],
 ["In w·x + b, b is the…",["Bias","Batch","Backprop","Baseline"]]]],
["Activation Functions",
"Activations add non-linearity. Without them, stacked layers collapse into a single linear layer.\n\nReLU = max(0, x) is a common default for hidden layers. Sigmoid squashes to 0–1, which suits probability outputs. tanh squashes to −1–1. GELU is common in BERT and GPT-2-era transformers; many modern LLMs (Llama, Mistral, Qwen) use SwiGLU, a gated SiLU unit in the feed-forward layer.",
[["ReLU(−3) = ?",["0","−3","3","0.05"]],
 ["Without activations, a 10-layer network equals…",["A single linear layer","A stack of ReLU layers","A sigmoid output unit","A deeper, more expressive model"]],
 ["The activation used in BERT and GPT-2?",["GELU","ReLU","Sigmoid","SwiGLU"],"BERT and GPT-2 use GELU; SwiGLU came later with Llama-style models."]]],
["Multilayer Perceptrons",
"An MLP stacks linear layers with activations between them: input → hidden → hidden → output. The hidden layers learn useful intermediate features.\n\nWith enough hidden units, even one hidden layer can approximate any continuous function on a closed, bounded input range.",
[["An MLP is linear layers plus…",["Non-linear activations","More linear layers","Wider hidden layers","Convolutions"]],
 ["Layers between input and output are called…",["Hidden layers","Pooling layers","Embedding tables","Output heads"]],
 ["Universal approximation means…",["Wide enough MLPs can approximate any continuous function","MLPs never overfit with enough data","Any MLP trains to the global minimum","One hidden unit can fit any smooth function"]]]],
["The Forward Pass",
"The forward pass pushes inputs through each layer to get predictions and then the loss:\n\n$h = relu(x @ W1 + b1)\ny = h @ W2 + b2\n\nIt keeps the intermediate values, because backprop needs them.",
[["The forward pass produces…",["Predictions and the loss","Gradients from backprop","Updated weights","Only the intermediate values"]],
 ["Why store activations during the forward pass?",["Backprop needs them for gradients","Only so they can be logged for debugging","To reuse them at inference","So the loss can be recomputed later"]],
 ["Does inference need backprop?",["No, just the forward pass","Yes, to compute the loss","Only when running on GPUs","Only for the first batch"]]]],
["Errors Flow Back",
"Backpropagation computes gradients from the loss backward through each layer using the chain rule. Each layer receives the gradient from the layer above, multiplies it by its own local derivative, and passes the result down.",
[["Backprop goes…",["From the loss back to the inputs","From the inputs forward to the loss","In random order across layers","From the first layer to the last"]],
 ["Each layer multiplies the incoming gradient by…",["Its local derivative","The gradient of the layer below","The current learning rate","The loss value"]],
 ["After backprop, what actually updates the weights?",["The optimizer step","The loss","The data loader","The backward pass itself"]]]],
["Crunching the Numbers",
"A tiny example: y = w·x and loss = (y − t)². With w = 2, x = 3, t = 5: y = 6 and loss = 1.\n\ndL/dy = 2(y − t) = 2, and dy/dw = x = 3, so dL/dw = 2 × 3 = 6. With a learning rate of 0.1, the new w = 2 − 0.6 = 1.4.",
[["y = w·x and loss = (y − t)², with w = 2, x = 3, t = 5. What is dL/dw?",["6","2","3","1"],"dL/dy = 2(y − t) = 2, dy/dw = x = 3, so dL/dw = 6."],
 ["w = 2 and dL/dw = 6. What is the new w with a learning rate of 0.1?",["1.4","2.6","1.94","1.6"]],
 ["With x = 3 and t = 5, w moves from 2 to 1.4, so y goes from 6 to 4.2. What happens to loss = (y − t)²?",["Falls from 1 to 0.64","Rises from 1 to 1.44","Stays at 1","Falls from 1 to 0"]]]],
["Backprop in Code",
"PyTorch does backprop for you:\n\n$opt.zero_grad()\nloss = loss_fn(model(x), y)\nloss.backward()   # compute gradients\nopt.step()        # update weights\n\nForgetting `zero_grad()` makes gradients pile up across steps.",
[["Which line computes the gradients?",["`loss.backward()`","`opt.step()`","`model(x)`","`opt.zero_grad()`"]],
 ["Forgetting `opt.zero_grad()` causes…",["Gradients to add up across steps","Gradients to reset to zero each step","Weights to stop updating","An error on the second step"]],
 ["Wrap inference code in…",["`torch.no_grad()`","`model.train()`","`opt.zero_grad()`","`torch.backward()`"]]]],
["Optimizers",
"SGD with momentum keeps a running velocity, so it rolls through small bumps. Adam adapts a step size for each parameter using averages of past gradients and squared gradients.\n\nAdamW is Adam with decoupled weight decay, and it’s the standard choice for training transformers. Weight decay shrinks the weights slightly toward zero each step; with plain SGD it’s equivalent to L2 regularization. ‘Decoupled’ means AdamW applies it directly to the weights instead of adding it to the gradient, where Adam’s adaptive step would rescale it.",
[["The standard optimizer for training transformers?",["AdamW","SGD without momentum","Newton’s method","Adagrad"]],
 ["Momentum helps by…",["Smoothing updates with past gradients","Raising the batch size over time","Scaling each weight’s own step size","Shrinking the learning rate each step"]],
 ["Adam’s key idea?",["An adaptive step size per parameter","One fixed step size shared by all weights","Gradient-free updates","A learning rate that only decays"]]]],
["Convolutions: The Window",
"A convolution slides a small filter, like 3×3, over an image and computes a dot product at each position. One filter detects one pattern, such as a vertical edge, anywhere in the image.\n\nThe same weights are reused at every position, so there are far fewer parameters than in a dense layer.",
[["Why does a conv layer have fewer parameters than a dense layer?",["One small filter is reused everywhere","Images are smaller than typical text inputs","It skips every other pixel","Each filter only sees one channel"]],
 ["A 3×3 filter on 1 channel has how many weights, ignoring bias?",["9","3","27","6"]],
 ["Conv layers are good at detecting…",["Local patterns wherever they appear","Patterns only at the center of the image","Long-range word order in text","Global image color statistics"]]]],
["Convolutions: Stacking Filters",
"A conv layer has many filters, each producing its own feature map; the number of filters is the number of output channels. A 3×3 filter over an RGB input has 3×3×3 = 27 weights.\n\nStacking layers builds a hierarchy: edges → textures → parts → objects.",
[["64 filters → how many output channels?",["64","3","1","192"]],
 ["Weights in one 3×3 filter over 3 input channels?",["27","9","3","81"]],
 ["Deeper conv layers detect…",["Larger, more complex patterns","Only edges","Only colors","Smaller, simpler patterns than early layers"]]]],
["Intro to CNNs",
"A CNN chains conv → activation → pooling blocks to extract features, then uses dense layers to classify. Well-known CNNs include LeNet, AlexNet, VGG, and ResNet.\n\nCNNs dominated computer vision until vision transformers arrived, and modern CNNs like ConvNeXt remain competitive with them. CNNs’ built-in locality helps on small datasets, and efficient CNNs like MobileNet are common on-device.\n\nA residual (skip) connection adds a block’s input to its output, y = x + F(x), so gradients reach early layers through the shortcut. That’s what let ResNet train networks over 100 layers deep (152 on ImageNet).",
[["A typical CNN block?",["Conv → ReLU → Pool","Pool → Softmax → Conv","Attention → LSTM","Softmax → Conv → ReLU"]],
 ["The final layers of a CNN classifier are usually…",["Dense layers and a softmax","More conv and pooling layers","Embedding lookups","Recurrent LSTM layers"]],
 ["ResNet’s key idea?",["Residual (skip) connections","Recurrent connections","Removing all convolutions","Much larger 11×11 filters in every layer"]]]],
["Pooling and Stride",
"Stride is how far the filter moves each step; stride 2 halves width and height (given suitable padding). Max pooling keeps the largest value in each window, shrinking the map and adding a little tolerance to shifts.\n\nOutput size = floor((W − K + 2P) / S) + 1, where K is the kernel size and P the padding.",
[["32×32 input, 2×2 max pool with stride 2 →",["16×16","32×32","8×8","31×31"]],
 ["Conv with W = 32, K = 3, P = 1, S = 1. Output width?",["32","30","16","34"]],
 ["Max pooling keeps…",["The largest value in each window","The average value in each window","The first value in each window","The sum of each window"]]]],
["Dropout and Batch Norm",
"Dropout zeroes random activations during training to stop neurons depending on each other; it’s off at inference. Batch norm normalizes activations using batch statistics, which stabilizes and speeds up training.\n\nCall `model.eval()` before inference so both behave correctly.",
[["Forgetting `model.eval()` at test time causes…",["Dropout stays on; BN uses batch stats","BN freezes its running averages","Gradients are computed but unused","Nothing; `eval()` only frees up some memory"]],
 ["Batch norm mainly…",["Stabilizes and speeds up training","Zeroes random activations","Adds batch statistics as features","Reduces the number of trainable parameters"]],
 ["Dropout at inference is…",["Off","On","Doubled","Halved"]]]],
["Building a Complete CNN",
"A common recipe: stacked conv blocks with growing channels (32 → 64 → 128) and shrinking spatial size, then global average pooling, then a linear layer to the number of classes.\n\nTrain with cross-entropy and AdamW, plus data augmentation and a validation set.",
[["Channels usually ___ as spatial size ___.",["grow; shrinks","shrink; grows","stay; stays","grow; grows"]],
 ["Global average pooling turns a (C, H, W) map into…",["A vector of length C","An (H, W) image","A single number","A vector of length H × W"]],
 ["The final layer’s output size equals…",["The number of classes","The image width","The batch size","The last channel count"]]]],
["Transfer Learning",
"Start from a model pretrained on a huge dataset like ImageNet and adapt it. With little data, freeze the backbone and train a new head. With more data, fine-tune all layers with a small learning rate.",
[["Small dataset, similar domain →",["Freeze the backbone, train a new head","Train the whole model again from scratch","Fine-tune every layer at a high rate","Retrain only the first conv layer"]],
 ["When fine-tuning all layers, use a…",["Small learning rate","Large learning rate","Learning rate of zero","Rate that grows each epoch"]],
 ["Why does transfer learning work?",["Early layers learn reusable features","Pretrained models memorize your data","Pretraining removes the need for labels","Bigger models always generalize better"]]]],
["CNN Shape Check",
"Practice tracking shapes. Input (1, 3, 224, 224). A conv with 64 filters, 3×3, padding 1 → (1, 64, 224, 224). MaxPool 2 → (1, 64, 112, 112).\n\nFlattening that gives 64 × 112 × 112 = 802,816 features, which is why we pool more or use global average pooling.",
[["(1, 3, 224, 224) → Conv(16 filters, 3×3, padding 1) →",["(1, 16, 224, 224)","(1, 3, 222, 222)","(1, 16, 222, 222)","(1, 48, 224, 224)"]],
 ["(1, 16, 224, 224) → MaxPool(2) →",["(1, 16, 112, 112)","(1, 8, 224, 224)","(1, 16, 224, 224)","(1, 8, 112, 112)"]],
 ["PyTorch image tensor order?",["(batch, channels, height, width)","(batch, height, width, channels)","(channels, batch, height, width)","(height, width, channels, batch)"]]]],
["Architectures: Boss Fight",
"Quick recap: linear layers plus activations make MLPs; backprop plus an optimizer trains them; convolutions share weights across an image; pooling shrinks; batch norm stabilizes; dropout regularizes; residual connections let networks go deep.",
[["Model stuck at 50% on a balanced binary task, loss flat →",["Check LR, labels and gradient flow","Add more layers and train for longer","Add dropout to every layer","Switch to a larger batch size"]],
 ["Which adds non-linearity?",["ReLU","Matrix multiplication","Flatten","Adding a bias"]],
 ["Skip connections mainly fix…",["Gradient flow in very deep nets","Overfitting on small training datasets","Slow data loading","Exploding learning rates"]]]]
]},

{t:"Classical ML Algorithms", ls:[
["Decision Trees",
"A tree asks yes/no questions about features (‘age > 30?’) and keeps splitting until the leaves make predictions. Splits are chosen to make the groups purer, measured by Gini impurity or entropy. Gini impurity is 1 − Σp² over the classes at a node: a 50/50 node scores 0.5, and a pure node scores 0. The tree tries every feature and threshold and keeps the split that lowers the weighted impurity most, greedily, one node at a time.\n\nTrees are easy to explain, but deep trees overfit. Limit depth or the minimum samples per leaf.",
[["Trees choose splits that…",["Leave each child group less mixed","Split on a randomly chosen feature","Use as many features as possible","Make the two children equal in size"]],
 ["How do you fix a tree that overfits?",["Cap its maximum depth","Let it grow deeper","Remove the root split","Standardize the features"]],
 ["Do trees need feature scaling?",["No","Yes, always","Only for the labels","Only for deep trees"],"Splits compare a feature against a threshold, so rescaling a feature doesn’t change which splits are possible."]]],
["Random Forests",
"A random forest trains many trees, each on a bootstrap sample of the data and considering a random subset of features at each split, then averages their votes. Averaging many de-correlated trees reduces variance. A bootstrap sample draws n rows with replacement, so each tree sees about 63% of the distinct rows, some more than once.\n\nIt’s a strong default for tabular data.",
[["Random forests mainly reduce…",["Variance","Bias","Training time","Number of features"]],
 ["Each tree trains on…",["A bootstrap sample of the rows","A random subset of features only","All rows, in the same order","The residuals of the previous tree"]],
 ["The final prediction is…",["A vote or average across trees","The answer of the best single tree","The answer of the deepest tree","A weighted sum fit by boosting"]]]],
["Gradient Boosting",
"Boosting builds trees one after another, with each new tree fixing the errors of the ones before it. Precisely, each tree fits the negative gradient of the loss for the current ensemble; for squared error, that’s just the residuals. Boosting mainly reduces bias, while random forests mainly reduce variance. XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost often win on tabular data.\n\nTune the learning rate and number of trees, and use early stopping.",
[["Each new boosted tree is fit to…",["The loss gradient: residuals for MSE","The errors of the first tree only","A bootstrap sample of the raw labels","The average of the previous trees"]],
 ["The go-to for tabular competitions?",["Gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM)","Deep CNNs on the raw table","K-means then a lookup","A single, fully grown decision tree per class"]],
 ["Compared to random forests, boosting trains trees…",["Sequentially","In parallel, independently","Only once","On disjoint subsets of rows"]]]],
["K-Nearest Neighbours",
"KNN stores the training data and predicts by looking at the k closest points. There’s no real training, but prediction is slow and sensitive to feature scale.\n\nA small k overfits; a large k oversmooths.",
[["KNN’s training cost?",["Almost none: it stores the data","Very high, like training a deep net","It needs gradient descent","It must build a tree first"]],
 ["Must you scale features for KNN?",["Yes, distances depend on scale","No, KNN is scale-invariant","Only the labels need to be scaled first","Only when k is large"]],
 ["k = 1 tends to…",["Overfit","Underfit","Be perfect","Oversmooth"]]]],
["Support Vector Machines",
"An SVM finds the boundary with the widest margin between classes. Only the support vectors, the points on the margin or on its wrong side, determine it.\n\nKernels like RBF let it draw curved boundaries by implicitly mapping data into higher dimensions.",
[["An SVM maximizes…",["The margin between classes","The number of support vectors","Training accuracy at any cost","The distance between class means"]],
 ["The kernel trick enables…",["Non-linear boundaries","Faster training on huge data","Unsupervised clustering","Automatic feature scaling"]],
 ["Support vectors are…",["Points on the margin or on its wrong side","Every training point, weighted equally","Only the outliers far away","The class centers"]]]],
["Naive Bayes",
"Naive Bayes applies Bayes’ rule while assuming the features are independent given the class. That assumption is ‘naive’, but it often works well for text tasks like spam detection.\n\nIt’s fast and needs little data.",
[["‘Naive’ refers to assuming…",["Features are independent given the class","The class is independent of the features","The training set is small and has no noise","Features are normally distributed"]],
 ["A classic use case?",["Spam and text classification","Image segmentation","Time-series forecasting of sales","Self-driving perception"]],
 ["Its main strength?",["Fast, and fine with little data","Captures complex feature interactions","Needs truly independent features to run","Best accuracy on any dataset"]]]],
["K-Means Clustering",
"K-means groups unlabeled data into k clusters: pick k centers, assign each point to its nearest center, move each center to its cluster’s mean, and repeat.\n\nChoose k with the elbow method or silhouette score, and scale features first.",
[["K-means is…",["Unsupervised clustering","Supervised classification","A neural network","Dimensionality reduction"]],
 ["The step after assigning points?",["Move each center to its points’ mean","Delete the farthest outliers","Merge the two closest clusters into one","Pick k new random centers"]],
 ["Choose k using…",["The elbow method or silhouette score","Validation accuracy against the true labels","The learning rate schedule","The number of features"]]]],
["PCA and Dimensionality Reduction",
"PCA finds new axes, the principal components, along which the data varies most, and keeps the top few. It compresses data, removes redundancy, and helps you visualize in 2D.\n\nStandardize first, since PCA is sensitive to scale.",
[["PCA keeps the directions of…",["Maximum variance","Minimum variance","Random directions","Best class separation"]],
 ["Is PCA supervised or unsupervised?",["Unsupervised","Supervised","Reinforcement learning","Self-supervised"]],
 ["Use PCA for…",["Compression and visualization","Adding labels to raw data","Adding more dimensions","Selecting the most predictive features"]]]],
["Classical ML Capstone",
"Picking a model: for tabular data, try gradient boosting first. If you need explainability, use logistic regression or a shallow tree. With tiny data, keep it simple. For images, text, and audio, use neural networks.\n\nAlways beat a simple baseline before going fancy.",
[["Tabular churn data with 50k rows →",["Gradient boosting","A CNN","K-means","KNN with k = 1 on raw rows"]],
 ["A regulator wants a fully explainable model →",["Logistic regression or a small tree","Gradient boosting with feature importances","A neural network with saliency maps","A random forest of 500 trees"]],
 ["Raw images →",["A CNN or vision transformer","Naive Bayes on pixels","Linear regression","Gradient boosting on pixels"]]]],
["Classical ML Review",
"Recap: trees split for purity, forests average to cut variance, boosting fixes errors in sequence, KNN votes by distance, SVMs maximize the margin, naive Bayes assumes independence, k-means clusters, and PCA compresses.",
[["Which needs no training step?",["KNN","Random forest","SVM","Naive Bayes"]],
 ["Which is a clustering algorithm?",["K-means","XGBoost","Logistic regression","PCA"]],
 ["Which reduces variance by averaging?",["Random forest","A single deep tree","KNN with k = 1","Gradient boosting"]]]],
["Customer Churn Predictor",
"A plan: define churn (for example, no activity in 30 days), build features from usage history up to a cutoff date, split by time, train gradient boosting, evaluate with PR-AUC, and pick a threshold based on how many customers the retention team can contact.",
[["Why split by time?",["So it never trains on the future","It makes training faster","Random splits aren’t allowed","It balances churners across splits"]],
 ["Which metric if churners are 5% of users?",["PR-AUC, or precision and recall","Accuracy","MSE","Training loss"]],
 ["The threshold should depend on…",["Team capacity and the cost of misses","The size of the model","The number of training epochs","The validation accuracy"]]]]
]},

{t:"RNNs & Sequence Models", ls:[
["Sequence Problems",
"Some data comes in order: text, speech, stock prices, sensor logs. Order matters, since ‘dog bites man’ isn’t ‘man bites dog’.\n\nShapes of tasks: many-to-one (sentiment), one-to-many (image captioning), and many-to-many (translation).",
[["Sentiment of a review is…",["Many-to-one","One-to-many","One-to-one","Many-to-many"]],
 ["Translation is…",["Many-to-many","Many-to-one","One-to-one","One-to-many"]],
 ["Why not use a plain MLP on text?",["It ignores order and needs fixed-size input","It can’t represent words as numbers","It has too few parameters to learn language well","It overfits any text with ease"]]]],
["Recurrent Neural Networks",
"An RNN reads a sequence one step at a time and keeps a hidden state that summarizes what it has seen so far: h_t = tanh(W·x_t + U·h_(t−1) + b).\n\nThe same weights are used at every step.",
[["The hidden state is…",["A running summary of the sequence","The final predicted label","The next input token","The weight matrix shared across steps"]],
 ["Are RNN weights shared across time steps?",["Yes","No","Only the first step","Only the output layer"]],
 ["RNNs process tokens…",["One after another","All at once in parallel","In random order","Last to first only"]]]],
["Vanishing and Exploding Gradients",
"Backprop through time multiplies one factor per step: the recurrent weight matrix times the tanh derivative (at most 1). When that product’s largest stretch factor (its largest singular value) is consistently below 1, gradients shrink geometrically (vanish) and long-range information is lost; above 1, they can explode. The LSTM’s cell state is updated by addition, which gives gradients a path that barely shrinks while the forget gate stays near 1.\n\nFixes: gradient clipping for exploding gradients, and gated cells like LSTMs and GRUs for vanishing ones.",
[["A fix for exploding gradients?",["Gradient clipping","Switching to GRU cells","A bigger learning rate","Removing the tanh"]],
 ["Vanishing gradients make RNNs forget…",["Long-range dependencies","Recent tokens","The labels","The first batch"]],
 ["Why does it happen?",["Repeated multiplication over many steps","Training on too much data","The softmax at the output","A learning rate that is set far too low"]]]],
["LSTMs and GRUs",
"LSTMs add a cell state, a kind of conveyor belt that carries information across many steps with little change, controlled by gates.\n\nGRUs are a simpler version with fewer gates and often similar performance.",
[["An LSTM’s long-term memory lives in the…",["Cell state","Hidden state","Output gate","Embedding"]],
 ["GRU compared to LSTM?",["Simpler, with fewer gates","More gates and parameters","Exactly the same","Has no gates at all"]],
 ["Gates mainly help fix…",["Vanishing gradients","Overfitting","Tokenization","Exploding weights"]]]],
["Opening the Gates",
"LSTM gates are sigmoids (0 to 1) that act like valves. The forget gate decides what to erase from the cell. The input gate decides what new information to write. The output gate decides what to reveal as the hidden state.",
[["Which gate decides what to erase?",["Forget gate","Input gate","Output gate","Reset gate"]],
 ["Gate values range from…",["0 to 1","−1 to 1","0 to infinity","−∞ to ∞"]],
 ["The output gate controls…",["What part of the cell becomes h_t","What gets erased from the cell state","What new information is written","How fast the cell state decays"]]]],
["Limits of Recurrence",
"RNNs must process tokens one at a time, so they can’t parallelize across a sequence and are slow to train. Memory still fades over very long sequences.\n\nAttention lets every token look at every other token directly, which is what led to transformers.",
[["The main speed problem with RNNs?",["They’re sequential, so no parallelism","They have too few parameters to scale","They don’t run on GPUs","Their hidden state is too large"]],
 ["What replaced RNNs for most language tasks?",["Transformers","CNNs","Decision trees","GRUs"]],
 ["Attention gives each token…",["Direct access to all other tokens","Access to the previous token only","A fixed-size summary of the past","No context beyond itself"]]]],
["Generating Text with an RNN",
"Character-level generation: feed in a seed, predict a probability for the next character, sample one, feed it back in, and repeat. Temperature controls randomness.\n\nTraining uses teacher forcing: feed the true previous token, not the model’s own guess.",
[["Teacher forcing feeds…",["The true previous token in training","The model’s own previous sampled guess","A random token from the vocab","The most likely next token"]],
 ["Higher temperature →",["More random, varied output","More repetitive, predictable output","Faster generation","Shorter output"]],
 ["The generation loop is…",["Predict, sample, append, repeat","Encode, decode, then sample once","Sample all tokens, then sort","Train, test, deploy"]]]]
]},

{t:"Transformers & LLMs", ls:[
["Word Embeddings",
"Words become vectors, learned so that similar meanings sit close together. word2vec famously showed arithmetic like king − man + woman ≈ queen.\n\nIn a transformer, an embedding table maps each token ID to a vector at the input.",
[["Words with similar meanings have embeddings that are…",["Close together","Identical","Always perpendicular","Far apart"]],
 ["An embedding table maps…",["Token IDs to vectors","Vectors to labels","Images to text","Raw words to integer token IDs"]],
 ["A classic word2vec example?",["king − man + woman ≈ queen","king · queen = 0","man + woman ≈ person","king − queen ≈ 0"]]]],
["Attention — Q, K, V",
"Attention lets each token pull information from other tokens. Each token makes a query (what am I looking for?), a key (what do I contain?), and a value (what do I pass along?).\n\nIn ‘The cat sat because it was tired’, a head can learn to make ‘it’ attend strongly to ‘cat’.",
[["A query represents…",["What a token is looking for","What a token contains","A token’s position","What a token passes along"]],
 ["Values are…",["The content that gets mixed and passed on","The match scores between tokens","The mask over future tokens","What each token is searching for in others"]],
 ["In self-attention, Q, K, and V come from…",["The same sequence","Different datasets","The labels","Encoder and decoder"]]]],
["Attention Scores & Softmax",
"Attention = softmax(QKᵀ / √d_k) · V. QKᵀ scores how well each query matches each key. Dividing by √d_k keeps the scores from getting so large that softmax saturates. Softmax turns scores into weights that sum to 1, and those weights mix the values.\n\nComparing every token with every other token makes the cost grow with the square of sequence length.",
[["The attention formula?",["softmax(QKᵀ / √d_k) · V","softmax(QVᵀ / √d_k) · K","softmax(QKᵀ · V) / √d_k","softmax(KVᵀ / d_k) · Q"]],
 ["Why divide by √d_k?",["Stop scores saturating the softmax","Make the attention run faster","Make every score positive","Normalize the values so they sum to 1"]],
 ["Attention cost grows with sequence length n as…",["n²","n","log n","n³"]]]],
["Multi-Head Attention",
"Instead of one attention, run several heads in parallel, each with its own Q, K, V projections. Different heads can focus on different relationships, like syntax or which noun a pronoun refers to.\n\nThe heads’ outputs are concatenated and projected back.",
[["Why use multiple heads?",["Each can learn different relationships","Heads need fewer parameters in total","It removes the need for softmax","Each head sees a different part of the input"]],
 ["Head outputs are…",["Concatenated, then projected","Averaged and discarded","Summed into one number","Passed on to separate output layers"]],
 ["d_model = 512 with 8 heads → each head’s dimension?",["64","512","8","4096"]]]],
["Positional Encoding",
"Attention on its own ignores order, so position information has to be added. The original transformer added fixed sine and cosine vectors to the embeddings.\n\nRoPE rotates Q and K by an angle based on position, so scores depend on relative distance. Most modern open LLMs (Llama, Mistral, Qwen) use it.",
[["Why is positional encoding needed?",["Attention alone ignores token order","To speed up training","To shrink the vocabulary","Embeddings alone can’t store word meaning"]],
 ["RoPE encodes…",["Relative position, via rotation","Absolute position, via addition","Word meaning","The batch index"]],
 ["The original transformer used…",["Fixed sine and cosine encodings","No positional information","Learned relative biases","An RNN over the inputs"]]]],
["The Transformer Block",
"A block is multi-head attention plus a feed-forward MLP, each wrapped with a residual connection and layer norm. Stack N of these blocks.\n\nDecoders add a causal mask so tokens can’t see the future. Modern LLMs normalize before each sublayer (pre-norm).\n\nThe residual connection adds each sublayer’s input back to its output (x + f(x)), so information and gradients flow straight through. Layer norm rescales each token’s vector to mean 0 and variance 1, then applies a learned scale and shift; RMSNorm skips the mean and just divides by the root mean square. This keeps activations stable.",
[["The two main sublayers?",["Attention and a feed-forward MLP","Attention and a causal mask","A residual and a layer norm","Convolution and pooling"]],
 ["Each sublayer is wrapped with…",["A residual connection and layer norm","A causal mask and dropout","Multi-head attention","Batch norm only"]],
 ["The causal mask is used in…",["Decoders","Encoders like BERT","Every multi-head layer","Feed-forward sublayers"]]]],
["Tokenisation & Context Windows",
"Text is split into tokens with BPE or a similar method. As a rule of thumb, an English token averages about 4 characters, though it varies by tokenizer. The context window is the maximum number of tokens the model can handle at once, prompt and output together.\n\nLonger contexts cost more: attention grows quadratically and the KV cache (every past token’s stored keys and values, covered in the Decoding lesson) grows linearly.",
[["The context window counts…",["Prompt and output tokens together","Only output tokens","Only words","Only the system prompt and user turns"]],
 ["One English token is roughly…",["4 characters","1 sentence","1 letter","2 words"],"OpenAI’s rule of thumb for English: 1 token ≈ 4 characters, or about ¾ of a word. It varies by tokenizer and language."],
 ["Why do LLMs struggle to count letters in a word?",["They see tokens, not characters","They are bad at all arithmetic","Their context is too short","They were never trained on spelling"]]]],
["Pre-training & Fine-tuning",
"Pretraining: next-token prediction on huge amounts of text, which teaches language and knowledge. Supervised fine-tuning (SFT): train on instruction → response examples. Preference tuning (RLHF or DPO): align the model with human preferences.",
[["The pretraining objective for GPT-style models?",["Next-token prediction","Instruction → response tuning","Preference ranking","Image classification"]],
 ["SFT data looks like…",["Instruction → response pairs","Unlabeled web text","Pairs of preferred and rejected answers","Random noise"]],
 ["The usual order?",["Pretrain → SFT → preference tuning","SFT → pretrain → preference tuning","Preference tuning → pretrain → SFT","Pretrain → preference tuning → SFT"]]]],
["Prompting & In-Context Learning",
"LLMs can pick up a task from examples in the prompt, with no weight updates. That’s in-context learning. Zero-shot gives only instructions; few-shot adds examples; chain-of-thought asks for step-by-step reasoning.\n\nClear instructions and an explicit output format help a lot.",
[["Few-shot prompting means…",["Including examples in the prompt","Fine-tuning briefly on a few examples","Running on only a few GPUs","Asking for step-by-step reasoning"]],
 ["Does in-context learning update the weights?",["No","Yes","Only the last layer","Only with few-shot"]],
 ["Asking the model to reason step by step is…",["Chain-of-thought prompting","RLHF","Distillation","Few-shot example prompting"]]]],
["LLM Evaluation & Pitfalls",
"Benchmarks can be contaminated when test questions leak into pretraining data. An LLM used as a judge has biases, like preferring longer answers or the first option shown.\n\nFor a real product, build a golden set from real use cases, add human review, and check for regressions on every change.",
[["Benchmark contamination means…",["Test questions leaked into training data","The model is too small for the benchmark","The benchmark has wrong labels","The judge model is biased"]],
 ["LLM judges tend to favor…",["Longer answers","Shorter answers","Answers from a different model family","Answers that admit uncertainty"]],
 ["The best evaluation for your product?",["A golden set from real use cases","Public leaderboard rankings only","Spot-checking a few chats by eye","An LLM judge with no human review"]]]],
["Decoding: Temperature, Top-p & the KV Cache",
"The model outputs a probability for every possible next token. Low temperature makes the top choice dominate; high temperature flattens the distribution. Top-p keeps only the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities add up to p.\n\nConcretely, the last layer outputs one logit per vocabulary token and the model samples from softmax(logits / T): T < 1 sharpens the distribution, T > 1 flattens it, and T → 0 approaches greedy decoding.\n\nThe KV cache stores past keys and values, so each new token doesn’t recompute them. It speeds up generation but uses lots of GPU memory.",
[["Temperature near 0 means…",["Nearly greedy, almost deterministic","Maximum randomness","An error","Uniform sampling over the top-p tokens"]],
 ["The KV cache stores…",["Past keys and values, to skip recompute","The model weights for fast loading","Recent prompts, so whole answers can be reused","Training data for later fine-tuning"]],
 ["Top-p = 0.9 samples from…",["The fewest tokens covering 90% of the mass","The top 9 most likely tokens","A random 90% of the vocabulary","Tokens with probability above 0.9"]]]],
["Financial Sentiment Fine-Tuner",
"A project plan: start from a pretrained encoder (a BERT variant), add a classification head for positive, neutral, and negative, fine-tune on labeled financial headlines with a small learning rate for a few epochs, and evaluate with macro-F1 on a held-out set.",
[["Why macro-F1?",["Each class counts equally, despite imbalance","It weights each class by its frequency in data","It is the only metric for 3 classes","It is the fastest metric to compute"]],
 ["A typical fine-tuning learning rate for a BERT-size model?",["About 2e-5","About 1.0","About 10","About 1e-9"],"A rule of thumb, not a fixed value: the BERT paper picked fine-tuning rates between 2e-5 and 5e-5."],
 ["Which model type suits classification?",["An encoder with a classification head","A diffusion model","A GAN","A decoder-only LLM with no fine-tuning"]]]]
]},

{t:"Responsible ML", ls:[
["What Is Bias in ML?",
"Bias here means systematic, unfair errors. Common sources: unrepresentative data, historical bias baked into labels, biased measurement, and feedback loops.\n\nA hiring model trained on past hires can learn and repeat past discrimination.",
[["A hiring model trained on biased past decisions…",["Learns and repeats that bias","Averages the bias out over time","Becomes fair once accuracy is high","Can’t be trained on that data"]],
 ["A face model trained mostly on one group →",["Lower accuracy for other groups","Equal accuracy for everyone","Higher accuracy for minority groups","Faster but noisier inference"]],
 ["Does removing the sensitive column fully fix bias?",["No, proxies like zip code still leak it","Yes, since the model can’t see the attribute","Yes, if you also balance the classes","Only for image models"]]]],
["Fairness and Trade-offs",
"Fairness metrics include demographic parity (equal positive rates across groups), equalized odds (equal true and false positive rates), and calibration.\n\nYou usually can’t satisfy all of them at once, so choose based on the harm you care about most.",
[["Equal positive prediction rates across groups is…",["Demographic parity","Equalized odds","Calibration","Equal opportunity"]],
 ["Equal true and false positive rates across groups is…",["Equalized odds","Demographic parity","Calibration","Predictive parity"]],
 ["Can every fairness metric be met at once?",["Generally no","Always, with enough data","Yes, if the model is accurate","Only for binary tasks"]]]],
["Privacy and Data",
"Collect only the personal data you need, get consent, anonymize carefully (re-identification is easier than you’d think), and control access. Models can memorize training data.\n\nTools: differential privacy, federated learning, and scrubbing personal information.",
[["A model leaking its training data is called…",["Memorization","Regularization","Distillation","Overfitting to noise"]],
 ["Training on devices without centralizing the data is…",["Federated learning","Transfer learning","Differential privacy","Bagging"]],
 ["Does removing names make data anonymous?",["Not necessarily; other fields re-identify","Yes, names are the only identifying field","Yes, if emails are removed too","Only for image datasets"]]]],
["Misuse and Safety Basics",
"Think about how a system could be misused or fail: prompt injection, jailbreaks, harmful outputs, over-reliance.\n\nMitigations include red-teaming, input and output filters, rate limits, human review for high-stakes actions, and monitoring.",
[["Hidden instructions in a webpage hijacking an agent is…",["Prompt injection","A jailbreak","A harmful output","Data drift"]],
 ["Deliberately attacking your own system to find problems is…",["Red-teaming","Output filtering","Rate limiting","A/B testing"]],
 ["High-stakes automated actions should have…",["Human review or approval","Lower temperature only","More detailed logging only","A longer system prompt"]]]],
["Fairness Audit",
"Audit steps: define the groups and harms, slice metrics by group (accuracy, false positive rate, false negative rate), find gaps, trace root causes in the data, mitigate (reweighting, more data, threshold changes), and document it all in a model card.",
[["After defining the groups and harms, the first measurement step?",["Slice metrics by group","Reweight the training data","Write the model card","Change the thresholds"]],
 ["A model card documents…",["Intended use, data, metrics and limits","Only the training code and final weights","The hyperparameter search history","The serving cost per request"]],
 ["A reasonable mitigation?",["More data for underrepresented groups","Drop the group column and retrain","Report only the overall accuracy","Raise the threshold for everyone"]]]]
]},

{t:"MLOps", ls:[
["From Notebook to Production",
"Move from notebooks to modules: split data loading, training, and evaluation into scripts; put hyperparameters in config files; write tests for data and code; serve the model through an API or batch job; and run it all in CI.",
[["Hyperparameters belong in…",["Config files, versioned with the code","Hard-coded in a notebook cell","Environment variables set by hand","Comments next to the training loop code"]],
 ["A good first production test?",["Check the input schema and shapes","Assert the final accuracy is 100%","Test the plotting code","Nothing until users complain"]],
 ["Notebooks are great for…",["Exploration","Running production services","Storing secrets","Scheduled retraining"]]]],
["Reproducibility and Seeds",
"Set random seeds for Python, NumPy, and PyTorch; pin library versions; version your data and code; and log every run’s config and metrics with a tool like MLflow or Weights & Biases.\n\nSome GPU operations are still slightly non-deterministic.",
[["Track experiments with…",["MLflow or Weights & Biases","Pinned requirements files","Random seeds in every script","A shared spreadsheet"]],
 ["Setting seeds makes runs…",["Mostly repeatable","Faster","More accurate","Fully bit-identical on GPU"]],
 ["Pinning versions in requirements.txt prevents…",["Silent changes from library updates","Overfitting","Data drift","Random seed differences"]]]],
["Data Versioning and Drift",
"Version datasets (DVC, lakeFS, or snapshots) so you know exactly what each model trained on. In production, inputs drift. Compare live feature distributions to training using PSI or a KS test, and alert on big shifts. PSI compares binned histograms of a feature in training and in production (a common rule of thumb: under 0.1 is stable, above 0.25 is a major shift); the KS test measures the largest gap between the two cumulative distributions.",
[["A tool for versioning data?",["DVC","PSI","Matplotlib","Flask"]],
 ["Measure feature drift with…",["PSI or a KS test","DVC snapshots","Training accuracy","The loss curve"]],
 ["Concept drift is…",["A shift in how inputs map to labels","A shift in the input features’ ranges over time","The dataset growing over time","Code changes in the pipeline"]]]],
["Monitoring and Eval in Prod",
"Monitor system metrics (latency, errors, throughput), data quality (nulls, value ranges), the prediction distribution, and model quality once labels arrive. Set up alerts and dashboards, and review real samples regularly.",
[["Labels arrive weeks later. What can you monitor now?",["Prediction and feature distributions","Nothing until labels arrive","The training loss","Accuracy computed on live traffic now"]],
 ["A system metric example?",["p99 latency","F1","Recall","Calibration error"]],
 ["A sudden jump in null values most likely means…",["An upstream pipeline bug","A better model","Overfitting","Concept drift"]]]],
["Serving and Latency",
"Serving options: a REST or gRPC API, batch jobs, or on-device. Speed-ups: batching requests, caching, quantization, distillation, ONNX or TensorRT, and GPUs.\n\nTrack p50 and p99 latency; slow tail requests hurt users.",
[["p99 latency means…",["99% of requests finish at or below this","The average latency","The fastest request","1% of requests are faster than this one"]],
 ["Make a model smaller and faster with…",["Quantization or distillation","Batching or caching","Adding more layers","Raising the precision to fp64"]],
 ["Batching requests on a GPU increases…",["Throughput","Model accuracy","The number of model parameters","Data drift"]]]],
["Debugging a Training Run",
"Loss turns into NaN → lower the learning rate, clip gradients, and check the data for NaNs. Loss stays flat → check the learning rate, the labels, that gradients flow, and that you call `optimizer.step()`.\n\nValidation much worse than training → overfitting, or a mismatch between train and validation preprocessing.",
[["Loss flat from the first step →",["Check the LR, labels and gradient flow","Train for many more epochs","Add dropout and weight decay","Collect a lot more training data first"]],
 ["Loss becomes NaN →",["Lower LR, clip gradients, check data","Raise the learning rate","Add more dropout","Restart with a new seed"]],
 ["Great on train, terrible on validation from the same distribution →",["Overfitting or a preprocessing mismatch","Underfitting","A healthy model","A learning rate that is far too low for the data"]]]],
["Production ML Pipeline",
"A full pipeline: ingest → validate data → build features → train → evaluate against the current model → register → deploy as a canary → monitor → retrain on a schedule or when drift is detected. Orchestrate it with Airflow, Kubeflow, or similar.",
[["Before deploying a new model, compare it to…",["The current production model","A random baseline only","Its own training loss","Nothing"]],
 ["An orchestration tool?",["Airflow","A canary release","A model registry","NumPy"]],
 ["Common retraining triggers?",["A schedule or detected drift","Every canary that passes","Each new model registered","Only after an outage"]]]]
]},

{t:"Generative & Frontier", ls:[
["Generative vs Discriminative",
"Discriminative models learn P(y | x): given an input, which label? Generative models learn the data distribution itself, P(x) or P(x, y), so they can create new samples like images, text, or audio.\n\nClassifiers can be either: naive Bayes is generative, because it models P(x | y) and P(y) and then applies Bayes’ rule. Logistic regression is its discriminative counterpart.",
[["A logistic-regression spam classifier is…",["Discriminative","Generative","Neither","Both"]],
 ["A model that creates new faces is…",["Generative","Discriminative","Clustering","Contrastive"]],
 ["Generative models learn…",["The data distribution","Only decision boundaries","P(y | x) only","Cluster centers"]]]],
["Autoencoders and VAEs",
"An autoencoder compresses the input into a small latent code (the encoder) and reconstructs it (the decoder). A VAE shapes the latent space into a smooth distribution, so you can sample new points and decode them into new data.\n\nVAE loss = reconstruction error + a KL divergence term. The KL term measures how far the encoder’s distribution over codes is from a standard normal. It’s 0 only when they match; keeping it small is what lets you sample a random code from a standard normal and decode it.",
[["The autoencoder bottleneck holds…",["A compressed latent code","The labels","The gradients","A random noise vector for sampling"]],
 ["The VAE’s extra loss term?",["KL divergence to a prior","Cross-entropy on labels","L1 on the weights","An adversarial loss"]],
 ["VAEs generate new data by…",["Decoding sampled latent points","Running the encoder network backward","Clustering the training data","Adding noise to real inputs"]]]],
["GANs One-Pager",
"A GAN pits two networks against each other: a generator makes fakes and a discriminator tries to spot them. Both improve together.\n\nResults can be sharp, but training is unstable and can suffer mode collapse, where the generator produces little variety.",
[["The two networks in a GAN?",["Generator and discriminator","Generator and decoder","Encoder and decoder","Teacher and student"]],
 ["Mode collapse means…",["Generator outputs lack variety","The discriminator always wins","The loss reaches zero","The generator copies its training inputs"]],
 ["GAN training is known for being…",["Unstable","Prone to blurry results","Convex","Slow but stable"]]]],
["Diffusion Intuition",
"Diffusion models learn to remove noise. In classic (DDPM) training, add noise to real images and teach the model to predict that noise. To generate, start from pure noise and denoise step by step. Many recent image and video models use flow matching instead: they learn a velocity that carries noise toward data along a near-straight path, so they can often sample in fewer steps.\n\nText prompts steer the result by conditioning the model on a text embedding. Classifier-free guidance strengthens that steer by comparing predictions with and without the prompt and pushing further in the prompt’s direction.",
[["Classic (DDPM) diffusion training teaches the model to…",["Predict the added noise","Classify real vs fake images","Compress images to a code","Predict the next pixel"]],
 ["Generation starts from…",["Pure noise","A real image","A canvas of zeros","A blurred image"]],
 ["Text-to-image models are guided by…",["Conditioning on a text embedding","A classifier on the final image","Random class labels","Retrieving similar images from a database"]]]],
["Latent Space Explorer",
"In a good latent space, nearby points decode to similar outputs, and moving along a direction can change one attribute, like adding a smile. Interpolating between two latent codes gives a smooth morph between two outputs.",
[["Interpolating between two latent codes gives…",["Smooth transitions between outputs","Random noise in between","An abrupt jump halfway","The average of all the training images"]],
 ["Nearby latent points decode to…",["Similar outputs","Opposite outputs","Random outputs","Identical outputs"]],
 ["A direction in latent space can correspond to…",["An attribute, like smiling","A specific training image","The decoder’s learning rate","The image resolution"]]]]
]},

{t:"Retrieval-Augmented Generation", ls:[
["Why LLMs Hallucinate",
"LLMs predict plausible next tokens, not verified facts. They hallucinate when knowledge is missing, outdated, or rare, because they’re trained to sound fluent either way. Their knowledge is frozen at the training cutoff.\n\nGrounding answers in retrieved sources and asking for citations reduces hallucinations.",
[["Why do hallucinations happen?",["They produce plausible, unchecked text","Their GPUs make arithmetic errors","Only bad tokenization causes it","They copy wrong facts from the user’s prompt"]],
 ["Reduce hallucinations by…",["Grounding answers in retrieved docs","Raising the temperature","Using shorter prompts","Fine-tuning on much more generic web text"]],
 ["A model’s knowledge after training is…",["Frozen at its training cutoff","Updated from retrieved sources","Updated live from the web","Unlimited"]]]],
["Embeddings as Search",
"Embed documents and the query as vectors; similar meanings land close together. Semantic search finds ‘car insurance’ for ‘auto coverage’ even with no words in common.\n\nKeyword search (BM25) often wins for exact terms like product codes or error IDs.",
[["Semantic search matches…",["Meaning","Exact characters only","Word order","Document length"]],
 ["Searching for an exact error code like ‘E1043’ works better with…",["Keyword search (BM25)","Embedding search only","A larger embedding model","A cross-encoder alone"]],
 ["The usual similarity measure?",["Cosine similarity","Accuracy","Variance","Edit distance"]]]],
["Vector Databases",
"Vector databases store embeddings and run fast approximate nearest neighbor search (HNSW, IVF), plus metadata filtering. Examples: pgvector, Pinecone, Weaviate, and the FAISS library.\n\nIndex settings trade recall for speed.",
[["Vector databases get their speed from…",["Approximate nearest neighbor indexes","Exact scans over every stored vector","Sorting rows by date","Caching every past query"]],
 ["The Postgres extension for vectors?",["pgvector","pgsql-ai","vecgres","pg_embed"]],
 ["Metadata filtering lets you…",["Restrict search, e.g. to one customer","Speed up model training","Change the stored embeddings","Rank results by their semantic meaning"]]]],
["Retrieval Strategies",
"Chunking: split documents into pieces of a few hundred tokens (200–800 is a common rule of thumb) with some overlap. Hybrid search: combine keyword and vector scores. Reranking: a cross-encoder rescores the top results (say, 50) for precision.\n\nRewriting vague queries also helps.",
[["A reranker is typically…",["A cross-encoder on query and doc","A bi-encoder embedding each one separately","BM25 on the top results","A random shuffle of the top k"]],
 ["Hybrid search combines…",["Keyword and vector search","Chunking and reranking","Two different LLMs","A cross-encoder and query rewriting"]],
 ["Chunks that are too large →",["Noisier retrieval and wasted context","Better recall in every case","Faster, cheaper answers","Lost context at the chunk boundaries"]]]],
["RAG in Practice",
"When a RAG system answers wrong, evaluate retrieval and generation separately. Check whether the right chunk appears in the top results (recall@k). If it’s missing, fix retrieval. If it’s there but buried mid-context among noisy chunks, the model can miss it (‘lost in the middle’), so rerank or use a smaller k. If it’s at the top and the answer is still wrong, fix the prompt or the model.\n\nTell the model to answer only from the provided context and cite it.",
[["Measure retrieval quality with…",["Recall@k on labeled questions","BLEU of the final answer","The generator’s training loss","Average answer length"]],
 ["The right chunk is ranked first, but the answer is still wrong →",["Fix the prompt or the generator","Fix the chunking","Add more documents","Increase k"]],
 ["A good RAG prompt instruction?",["Answer only from the context, citing it","Use your own knowledge if unsure","Keep answers short and confident","Ignore sources that disagree"]]]]
]},

{t:"AI Agents", ls:[
["What Are Agents",
"An agent is an LLM in a loop that takes actions with tools (search, code, APIs), observes the results, and decides the next step until the goal is met.\n\nMore autonomy brings more capability and more ways to fail, since errors can compound over many steps.",
[["An agent is an LLM plus…",["Tools and a loop","More autonomy only","A bigger context only","A fine-tuned head"]],
 ["An agent decides its next step based on…",["Its goal and what it has observed","A fixed script written in advance","A random choice among tools","The user’s last message only"]],
 ["A key risk with agents?",["Errors compounding over many steps","They can’t call external tools or APIs","They’re too cheap to run","They can’t read tool output"]]]],
["Function Calling",
"You describe tools with a name, a description, and a parameter schema. The model replies with a structured call, like `get_weather(city='Paris')`. Your code runs the tool and sends the result back to the model.",
[["In standard function calling, who executes the tool?",["Your application code","The LLM itself","The user","The tokenizer"]],
 ["Tools are described with…",["A name, description and parameter schema","Several screenshots of the tool being used","Free-text instructions only","Its source code"]],
 ["After running the tool, you…",["Send the result back to the model","End the conversation","Retrain the model","Show the user and stop"]]]],
["The ReAct Pattern",
"ReAct interleaves reasoning and acting: Thought → Action → Observation → Thought… until done. Reasoning helps choose the right tool, and observations ground the next thought. The trace also makes failures easier to debug.",
[["The ReAct loop order?",["Thought → Action → Observation","Action → Thought → Observation","Observation → Action → Thought","Thought → Observation → Action"]],
 ["Observations come from…",["Tool results","The model’s own last thought","The training data","The system prompt"],"Observations come from outside the model, which is what grounds the next thought."],
 ["A benefit of explicit reasoning?",["Better tool choices, debuggable traces","Fewer tokens per task","No tools needed","Guaranteed correct answers on every task"]]]],
["Agent Memory",
"Short-term memory is the context window: the conversation plus tool results. Long-term memory lives outside the model, in a vector store or database the agent writes to and reads from.\n\nSummarize older turns to stay within the context window.",
[["Short-term memory is…",["The context window","A vector database","The model weights","A key-value cache on disk"]],
 ["Long-term memory usually uses…",["A vector store or database","The model’s context window","A bigger batch size","The KV cache"]],
 ["The context is getting too long →",["Summarize older turns","Delete the system prompt","Raise the temperature","Drop the tool results"]]]],
["Evaluating Agents",
"Measure task success rate on realistic tasks, steps and cost per task, tool-call errors, and safety (did it take harmful actions?). Log full traces of thoughts, tool calls, and results so you can debug failures.",
[["The primary agent metric?",["Task success rate","Tool-call error count","Steps per task","Perplexity"]],
 ["To debug agent failures, log…",["Full traces: thoughts, calls, results","Only the final answer","Only the errors raised","Only the final answer and the total cost"]],
 ["Also track…",["Cost and steps per task","Model parameter count","Number of GPUs","Prompt length only"]]]]
]},

{t:"Reinforcement Learning", ls:[
["Agents, Environments, Rewards",
"In RL, an agent takes actions in an environment, receives rewards, and learns a policy that maximizes expected total (discounted) future reward. There are no labeled answers, only feedback from consequences.\n\nThe discount factor γ makes near-term rewards count more than distant ones. A reward k steps away is weighted γ^k, so the effective horizon is about 1/(1 − γ) steps: with γ = 0.99, about 100 steps, and a reward 100 steps out counts 0.99¹⁰⁰ ≈ 0.37.",
[["RL learns from…",["Rewards from its actions","Labeled examples","Clusters","Human demonstrations only"]],
 ["A policy maps…",["States to actions","Actions to rewards","States to values","Labels to features"]],
 ["γ = 0.99 means future rewards…",["Shrink by 0.99× each step","Don’t count at all","Count exactly as much as now","Are cut off entirely after 99 steps"]]]],
["Explore vs Exploit & Q-Learning",
"Q-learning estimates Q(s, a), the expected discounted future reward of taking action a in state s and acting optimally after, and updates it toward reward + γ · max Q(next state).\n\nε-greedy mostly picks the best-known action but sometimes acts randomly to explore.",
[["ε-greedy with ε = 0.1 →",["10% random actions","10% best actions","90% random actions","Never explores"]],
 ["Q(s, a) estimates…",["Expected future reward of a in s","The probability of being in state s","The immediate reward of a in s","The best action to take in s"]],
 ["Pure exploitation risks…",["Missing better options","Too much randomness","Overfitting the labels","Unstable Q-value updates"]]]],
["Policy Gradients & PPO",
"Instead of learning values, policy gradient methods adjust the policy directly: make actions that led to high reward more likely.\n\nPPO is a stable policy gradient method that limits how far each update can move the policy. It’s been widely used in RLHF.",
[["Policy gradients increase the probability of…",["Actions that led to high reward","Random actions","All actions equally","Actions with high Q-values only"]],
 ["PPO is known for…",["Stable, clipped updates","Learning a Q-table","Needing no reward signal","Training without a policy"]],
 ["PPO has been widely used to fine-tune LLMs in…",["RLHF","PCA","K-means","Pretraining"]]]],
["RL for LLMs",
"RLHF: collect human preference comparisons, train a reward model on them, then optimize the LLM against it with a KL penalty to stay close to the original. Reward hacking is when the model exploits flaws in the reward model; the KL penalty limits it but doesn’t prevent it.\n\nDPO skips the reward model and RL loop, training directly on preference pairs.\n\nKL divergence measures how different two probability distributions are: it’s 0 when the tuned model gives every token the same probability as the original in every context, and it grows as they drift apart.",
[["The reward model learns from…",["Human preference comparisons","Outputs of the KL penalty","Labeled next tokens","Q-tables from games"]],
 ["The KL penalty limits…",["Drift from the original model","Overfitting to the labels","Slow inference","How often the reward model is retrained"]],
 ["What makes DPO different?",["No reward model or RL loop","It needs far more GPUs","It needs no preference data","It trains the reward model only"]]]]
]},

{t:"Fine-tuning in Practice", ls:[
["Fine-tune vs Prompt vs RAG",
"Try prompting first: it’s cheap and fast. Use RAG when the model needs fresh or private knowledge. Fine-tune when you need a consistent style or format, a specialized skill, or lower cost from a smaller model.\n\nThese combine well.",
[["The model needs your company’s latest docs →",["RAG","Fine-tuning only","Higher temperature","A longer system prompt"]],
 ["You need a consistent output format on a narrow task →",["Fine-tuning, after strong prompting","RAG over example outputs","Nothing, just raise temperature","Full pretraining on the narrow task data"]],
 ["The first thing to try?",["Prompting","RAG","Fine-tuning a small model","Full pretraining"]]]],
["LoRA Intuition",
"Full fine-tuning updates every weight. LoRA freezes the base model and learns small low-rank matrices (A × B) added to certain layers, so you train a tiny fraction of the parameters and can swap adapters per task.\n\nQLoRA does the same on top of a 4-bit quantized base model to save even more memory. Quantizing stores each weight in fewer bits (4-bit instead of 16-bit), cutting weight memory about 4× at a small accuracy cost.",
[["LoRA trains…",["Small low-rank adapter matrices","All of the base model’s weights","Only the embedding layer","Only the output head"]],
 ["QLoRA adds…",["A 4-bit quantized base model","More trainable layers","Higher precision adapters","A larger rank"]],
 ["The main benefit?",["Far less memory, swappable adapters","It always beats full fine-tuning on quality","It needs no training data","It makes inference much faster"]]]],
["Training Data for Fine-tuning",
"Quality beats quantity. A few hundred to a few thousand clean, diverse, representative examples often work. Match the format you’ll use in production, remove duplicates and errors, and hold out an evaluation set.",
[["What matters most?",["Clean, representative examples","As many examples as possible, any quality","Random web text","Only the hardest examples"]],
 ["Always hold out…",["An evaluation set","The duplicates","The longest examples","The system prompt"]],
 ["The training format should match…",["How you’ll prompt it in production","A general chat format","Whatever the base model saw","Anything; the format doesn’t matter much"]]]],
["Fine-tuning Evaluation",
"Compare the fine-tuned model to the base model (and a prompting baseline) on a held-out set, using task metrics plus LLM-judge or human review. Check for catastrophic forgetting on general tasks, and watch for rising validation loss.",
[["Catastrophic forgetting is…",["Losing general skills after fine-tuning","Forgetting the system prompt","Losing a saved checkpoint","Validation loss rising late in training"]],
 ["Always compare against…",["The base model and a prompt baseline","A random model only","A larger fine-tuned model from a vendor","Nothing"]],
 ["Validation loss rising during fine-tuning →",["Overfitting; stop earlier","Train for longer","Raise the learning rate","Underfitting; add layers"]]]]
]},

{t:"Practical ML Engineering", ls:[
["The HuggingFace Ecosystem",
"The Hugging Face Hub hosts models and datasets. Key libraries: `transformers` for models, `datasets` for loading data, `tokenizers`, `peft` for LoRA, `accelerate` for multi-GPU, and `evaluate` for metrics.\n\nModel cards describe usage and limitations.",
[["Which library handles LoRA?",["peft","datasets","accelerate","evaluate"]],
 ["Which library loads datasets?",["datasets","accelerate","tokenizers","torchvision"]],
 ["Where are models shared?",["The Hugging Face Hub","PyPI","npm","Docker Hub"]]]],
["Loading and Running Models",
"$from transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    name, dtype='auto', device_map='auto')\nids = tok('Hello', return_tensors='pt').to(model.device)\nout = model.generate(**ids, max_new_tokens=50)\nprint(tok.decode(out[0]))",
[["The class for a GPT-style model?",["AutoModelForCausalLM","AutoModelForMaskedLM","AutoTokenizer","AutoModelForSequenceClassification"]],
 ["`device_map='auto'` does what?",["Spreads layers over available devices","Picks the fastest dtype for the GPU","Downloads the weights to a local cache","Moves the input IDs to the GPU"]],
 ["Turn output IDs back into text with…",["`tok.decode`","`model.encode`","`print(ids)`","`tok.convert_ids`"]]]],
["Tokenizers in Depth",
"Tokenizers convert text to IDs and back, and add special tokens like BOS, EOS, and PAD. Padding makes a batch the same length; the attention mask tells the model to ignore the padding.\n\nChat models need their chat template: `tok.apply_chat_template(messages)`.",
[["The attention mask marks…",["Real tokens versus padding","The most important words in the input","Future tokens to hide","Tokens to use as labels"]],
 ["Format chat messages correctly with…",["`apply_chat_template`","`str.join`","`json.dumps`","`tok.encode`"]],
 ["The EOS token means…",["End of sequence","Error","Embedding","Empty output slot"]]]],
["Pipelines and Inference",
"`pipeline('sentiment-analysis')` wraps the tokenizer, model, and postprocessing in one call. For speed: batch inputs, use half precision, run under `torch.no_grad()`, and serve LLMs with engines like vLLM or SGLang (continuous batching, paged KV cache).",
[["`pipeline()` bundles…",["Tokenizer, model and postprocessing","Only the model weights","Only the dataset loader","Model, optimizer and the training loop"]],
 ["A high-throughput LLM serving engine?",["vLLM","`pipeline()`","pandas","scikit-learn"]],
 ["Run inference without tracking gradients using…",["`torch.no_grad()`","`model.train()`","`opt.zero_grad()`","`loss.detach()`"]]]],
["Putting It Together",
"A portfolio project: pick a dataset from the Hub, fine-tune a small model with LoRA, evaluate it against the base model, push the adapter to the Hub, and serve it behind a simple API. That’s a strong piece for MLE applications.",
[["A strong portfolio project?",["End to end: data, tuning, eval, deploy","A notebook with the best training loss","A model trained from scratch on a toy set","A long README describing ideas"]],
 ["Share your adapter via…",["The Hugging Face Hub","Email attachments","A PDF report","A shared drive"]],
 ["Before claiming an improvement, show…",["Evaluation against the base model","Training loss going down","The size of your GPU","Strong results on your own training data"]]]]
]},

{t:"System Design", ls:[
["How to Run the Interview",
"A solid flow: clarify functional and non-functional requirements (scale, latency, availability), estimate scale, define the API, sketch the data model, draw the high-level design, deep-dive on bottlenecks, and discuss trade-offs.\n\nThink out loud the whole way.\n\np99 latency is the time that 99% of requests finish within; the slowest 1% are the tail users notice. Back-of-envelope estimates get their own lesson near the end of this unit.",
[["The first step?",["Clarify requirements","Estimate scale","Define the API","Draw the high-level design"]],
 ["A non-functional requirement?",["p99 latency under 200 ms","Users can post photos","Users can like posts","Users can follow other users"]],
 ["Interviewers value…",["Trade-off reasoning out loud","Silent, perfect diagrams","Naming many technologies","Jumping straight to the design"]]]],
["Vertical vs Horizontal Scaling",
"Vertical scaling means a bigger machine. It’s simple but has a ceiling and is a single point of failure. Horizontal scaling means more machines behind a load balancer. It scales further but needs stateless services or shared state.\n\nKeep web servers stateless by storing sessions in a shared store like Redis.",
[["Why do horizontally scaled services stay stateless?",["Any instance can serve any request","Stateless servers use much less memory","Load balancers require it","It makes each request faster"]],
 ["The limit of vertical scaling?",["The biggest machine you can buy","There’s no real limit","Network bandwidth between servers","The number of load balancers"]],
 ["Where do sessions go when scaling horizontally?",["A shared store like Redis","On the load balancer","On one bigger machine","Each server’s local memory"]]]],
["Load Balancers",
"A load balancer spreads requests across servers and uses health checks to skip broken ones. Strategies: round robin, least connections, or hashing on user ID.\n\nLayer 4 balancers route by IP and port. Layer 7 balancers can route by URL or header, like /api to one pool and /images to another.",
[["Health checks…",["Keep traffic off unhealthy servers","Speed up database queries","Cache common responses","Encrypt traffic between servers"]],
 ["Routing /api and /images to different pools needs a…",["Layer 7 load balancer","Layer 4 load balancer","DNS round robin","Sticky-session setting"]],
 ["Avoid the load balancer being a single point of failure by…",["Redundant balancers with failover","Buying one bigger balancer","Restarting it on a schedule","Moving it behind the primary database"]]]],
["Caching",
"A cache keeps hot data in fast memory, like Redis, to cut latency and database load. Cache-aside: check the cache; on a miss, read the database and write the result to the cache. When full, an eviction policy like LRU drops the least recently used items.\n\nCache stampede: a hot key expires and many requests hit the database at once.",
[["Cache-aside on a miss →",["Read the DB, then fill the cache","Return an error to the client","Write a placeholder to the DB","Wait for the cache to refill itself"]],
 ["LRU evicts…",["The least recently used item","The oldest created item","The largest item","The least frequently used item"]],
 ["Prevent a cache stampede with…",["One-refill lock or jittered expiry","Shorter expiry on every key","Turning the cache off during peak traffic","A bigger cache with LRU"]]]],
["CDNs",
"A CDN is a global network of edge servers that cache static content like images, video, and scripts close to users. It cuts latency and takes load off your origin servers.\n\nDon’t cache highly personal or fast-changing data on a CDN.",
[["A CDN serves content from…",["Edge servers near users","Your primary database","The user’s own device storage","One central origin"]],
 ["Good CDN content?",["Images, video and JS bundles","Personal, fast-changing data","Account balances","Session tokens"]],
 ["A benefit besides lower latency?",["Less load on your origin","Stronger consistency","A smaller database","Simpler cache invalidation"]]]],
["SQL vs NoSQL",
"SQL databases (Postgres, MySQL) have schemas, joins, and ACID transactions, which suit related data where correctness matters. NoSQL covers key-value, document, wide-column, and graph stores; many give up joins, and often multi-row transactions or strong consistency, in exchange for flexible schemas and easier horizontal scaling. The line is blurring: distributed SQL like Spanner and CockroachDB scales out, and DynamoDB offers transactions.\n\nPayments → SQL. A massive stream of chat messages → often a wide-column store like Cassandra.",
[["Money transfers →",["SQL with ACID transactions","An eventually consistent store","A cache in front of a queue","A document store without transactions"]],
 ["A massive, write-heavy event log →",["A wide-column store like Cassandra","A single Postgres server","A CDN","A graph database"]],
 ["A typical NoSQL advantage?",["Easy horizontal scaling, flexible schemas","Powerful joins across many related tables","Strict schemas enforced on write","Strong ACID transactions by default"]]]],
["Sharding",
"Sharding splits a dataset across many database machines. A shard key decides where each row lives. A good key spreads load evenly; a skewed one creates hot shards.\n\nShard users by a hash of user ID, not by country.",
[["A good shard key spreads…",["Load evenly","Data by country","Everything into one shard","Related users together"]],
 ["What gets harder after sharding?",["Cross-shard joins and transactions","Single-key lookups","Reads by primary key","Writes for one user"]],
 ["A hot shard is often caused by…",["A skewed key, like one celebrity user","Hashing user IDs evenly across shards","Adding read replicas","Too many shards overall"]]]],
["Replication",
"Replication keeps copies of data on several machines. In leader-follower setups, writes go to the leader and are copied to followers, which serve reads. With asynchronous copying, followers can lag behind.\n\nRead-your-writes: send a user’s reads to the leader for a short time after they write.",
[["In leader-follower replication, writes go to…",["The leader","Any follower","The CDN","All nodes at once"]],
 ["A user sees stale data right after updating. Likely cause?",["Replication lag","Leader failure","Sharding","Cache too small"]],
 ["Guarantee users see their own writes by…",["Read from the leader briefly after a write","Add more followers","Block every write while a read is in flight","Shorten cache expiry"]]]],
["CAP Theorem",
"When a network partition splits a distributed system, it must choose: stay consistent by refusing some requests, or stay available by answering with possibly stale data. Partitions will happen, so the real choice is consistency versus availability during one.",
[["During a partition you choose between…",["Consistency and availability","Partition tolerance and consistency","Speed and cost","SQL and NoSQL"]],
 ["A bank ledger leans toward…",["Consistency","Availability","Neither","Partition tolerance"]],
 ["Like counts on posts lean toward…",["Availability","Consistency","Neither","Strict ordering"]]]],
["Message Queues",
"A message queue (Kafka, SQS, RabbitMQ) lets one service hand off work to another asynchronously. It decouples services, absorbs traffic spikes, and makes retries easy.\n\nWith at-least-once delivery, messages can repeat, so consumers should be idempotent or deduplicate. An idempotent consumer produces the same result if it handles a message twice, for example by recording processed message IDs and skipping repeats.",
[["Queues help…",["Decouple services and absorb spikes","Lower the storage needed","Make calls synchronous","Guarantee exactly-once delivery by default"]],
 ["At-least-once delivery requires…",["Idempotent consumers","A bigger queue","No retries","A single consumer"]],
 ["Transcoding a video after upload →",["Enqueue a background job","Do it inside the request","Run it on the client","Poll a cron job daily"]]]],
["Rate Limiting",
"Rate limiting caps how many requests a client can make. With a token bucket, each client’s bucket refills at a steady rate and each request spends a token; an empty bucket means the request is rejected with HTTP 429 (or delayed). Short bursts are fine, but the average rate is capped.\n\nWith many servers, keep counters in a shared store like Redis.",
[["A token bucket allows…",["Short bursts, capped average rate","No bursts at all, only a steady rate","Unlimited traffic after a warm-up","A fixed count per calendar minute"]],
 ["The standard HTTP status for a client over its rate limit?",["429","404","500","401"],"RFC 6585 defines 429 Too Many Requests for rate limiting. 503 signals server overload, though some services (like S3’s SlowDown) also use it for throttling."],
 ["With 50 API servers, keep counters in…",["A shared store like Redis","Each server’s own local memory","The client’s cookies","The load balancer logs"]]]],
["Consistent Hashing",
"With key mod N, changing N remaps almost every key. Consistent hashing puts servers and keys on a ring, and each key belongs to the next server clockwise. Adding or removing a server only moves nearby keys.\n\nVirtual nodes give each server many spots on the ring to balance load.",
[["Adding a node with consistent hashing moves…",["Only nearby keys","All keys","No keys","Half of all keys"]],
 ["Virtual nodes help…",["Balance load","Remap every key","Shrink the ring","Encrypt data"]],
 ["The problem with key mod N?",["Changing N remaps most keys","It’s too slow to compute per request","It only works for strings","It needs a prime number of servers"]]]],
["Back-of-Envelope Estimates",
"A day is about 100,000 seconds (86,400). Turn daily volume into requests per second, then multiply by 2–5× for peak. Storage = items per day × size × retention.\n\nExample: 100M daily users × 10 requests = 1B a day ≈ 10k requests per second on average (11.6k using the exact 86,400).",
[["Seconds in a day, roughly?",["100k (86,400)","1M","10k","1.44k"]],
 ["1B requests a day is about how many per second?",["10k","1M","100","100k"]],
 ["10M uploads a day at 2 MB each =",["20 TB a day","2 TB a day","200 GB a day","200 TB a day"]]]],
["Design a URL Shortener",
"Requirements: shorten a URL, redirect fast, maybe 100M new links a month, with far more reads than writes. Generate codes with base62 of a unique ID (from a counter or ID service) or a hash with collision checks.\n\nStore code → URL in a key-value store, cache hot codes, and return 301 or 302 redirects.",
[["7-character base62 codes give about…",["3.5 trillion","62 thousand","7 million","434 billion"]],
 ["The read/write pattern?",["Read-heavy, so cache aggressively","Write-heavy, so batch every insert","Balanced reads and writes","Write-heavy, so shard by code"]],
 ["Good storage for code → URL?",["A key-value store","A counter service","A cache with no database","A graph database"]]]]
]},

{t:"ML System Design", ls:[
["A Framework for ML Design Rounds",
"A solid order: clarify the goal and constraints, define offline and online metrics, data and labels, features, model (start simple), training and evaluation, serving and latency, then monitoring and iteration.\n\nInterviewers reward structure and clear trade-offs more than fancy models.",
[["Why start with a simple baseline?",["Sets a benchmark; easy to ship and debug","Interviewers forbid deep learning outright","Simple models are always the best","Deep models can’t be served fast"]],
 ["For ‘design a video recommender’, start by…",["Asking about scale, latency and success","Choosing a transformer architecture first","Drawing the database schema","Listing every feature you might use"]],
 ["Interviewers reward…",["Structure and trade-off reasoning","The most advanced model architecture","Speed of drawing diagrams","Naming the newest papers"]]]],
["Offline vs Online Metrics",
"Offline metrics come from held-out data: AUC, NDCG, log loss. Online metrics measure real impact: click-through rate, watch time, revenue, retention.\n\nA better offline score doesn’t guarantee an online win, so confirm with an A/B test.",
[["An online metric?",["Click-through rate","AUC","Log loss","NDCG"]],
 ["A reason offline gains don’t show online?",["Offline labels miss the real goal","Too much training data","GPUs differ between stages","Offline metrics are always too noisy"]],
 ["Confirm a new model’s real impact with…",["An A/B test","A higher offline AUC","Better NDCG on held-out data","More epochs"]]]],
["Two-Stage Recommenders",
"Scoring billions of items with a heavy model per request is too slow. So: candidate generation narrows billions to hundreds (often two-tower embeddings plus nearest-neighbor search), a ranking model scores those hundreds with rich features, and a re-ranking step adds rules like diversity and freshness.\n\nAn embedding is a learned vector per user and per item; a two-tower model scores a pair by the dot product of their vectors, so item vectors can be precomputed and searched with an approximate nearest-neighbor (ANN) index in milliseconds.",
[["Candidate generation must be…",["Cheap enough for huge catalogs","The most accurate stage","Run offline only","Scored by the heaviest model"]],
 ["The ranking model scores…",["Hundreds of candidates, rich features","The entire catalog, on every single request","Only newly added items","Billions of items, cheaply"]],
 ["Re-ranking adds…",["Rules like diversity and freshness","More candidates pulled from the index","New training labels","Scores for the full catalog"]]]],
["Feature Stores & Training-Serving Skew",
"Training-serving skew happens when features are computed differently in training (batch SQL) and serving (live code), so the model quietly gets worse. A feature store defines each feature once and serves it for both.\n\nPoint-in-time correctness: each training example uses feature values as they were at that moment, never later ones.",
[["Training-serving skew is…",["Features computed differently in each","Too little training data for the task","Slow serving latency","Labels drifting over time"]],
 ["A feature store helps by…",["One definition for training and serving","Storing model weights by version number","Labeling data automatically","Caching model predictions"]],
 ["Point-in-time correctness prevents…",["Future data leaking into training","Slow feature queries","Overfitting to labels","Skew from two different feature code paths"]]]],
["Batch vs Real-Time Inference",
"Batch inference precomputes predictions on a schedule and stores them: simple and cheap per prediction, but stale, and new users or items get nothing until the next run. Real-time inference predicts on each request with fresh context: current, but it needs fast serving.\n\nA daily recommendations email is batch. Search ranking for a typed query is real-time.",
[["A daily ‘recommended for you’ email →",["Batch inference","Real-time inference","No model","Streaming inference"]],
 ["Search ranking that depends on the typed query →",["Real-time inference","Batch inference","Monthly retraining","Precomputed daily scores"]],
 ["The main downside of batch inference?",["Predictions can be stale","It needs fast serving","It can’t be used with tree models","It’s hard to build"]]]],
["A/B Testing Models",
"Randomly split users into control (current model) and treatment (new model) and compare online metrics. Decide the metric, minimum effect, and sample size in advance, and run long enough to cover weekly patterns.\n\nWatch guardrail metrics like latency and complaints too.",
[["Why not stop as soon as p < 0.05?",["Peeking inflates false positives","It’s too slow to reach significance","It wastes the remaining data","p-values only work at the end of the week"]],
 ["Guardrail metrics include…",["Latency and crash rate","Training loss","Learning rate","Offline AUC"]],
 ["Run the test long enough to cover…",["Weekly patterns","One hour","One user","A single day"]]]],
["Cold Start & Labels",
"Cold start means no history for a new user or item. Use content features, signup signals, popular fallbacks, and exploration.\n\nImplicit feedback (clicks, watch time) is plentiful but biased by what was shown; top-ranked items get more clicks just for being on top. That’s position bias.",
[["A new item has zero clicks. A two-tower model can still embed it using…",["Content features like title, category","Its learned ID embedding","Its click history so far","Nothing until it gets its first clicks"]],
 ["Position bias means…",["Top slots get clicks for being on top","Users always prefer newer items","Labels arrive weeks late","Popular items get recommended more often"]],
 ["Implicit feedback examples?",["Clicks and watch time","Signup survey answers","Star ratings","Content features"]]]],
["Shadow & Canary Deployment",
"In shadow mode, the new model runs on live traffic but its outputs aren’t used, so you can compare it safely. In a canary, a small slice of real traffic goes to the new model, expanding if metrics look good.\n\nAlways keep a fast rollback.",
[["Shadow mode means…",["Runs on live traffic, outputs unused","Only 5% of users get to see its outputs","It runs offline on last week’s logs","It replaces the old model at night"]],
 ["A canary release sends the new model…",["A small slice of real traffic","All traffic at once","Only synthetic traffic","A copy of traffic, outputs unused"]],
 ["Always have…",["A fast rollback plan","A bigger model","More features","A second region"]]]]
]}

];
